**38** claims not in the schema, of which these survive classification:
L0006 S: The silent-listener helper binds a socket, accepts connections, and keeps every stream open without writing a byte. 
P: The silent-listener helper binds a socket, accepts connections, and keeps every stream open without writing a byte. A closed port would not do here, because it fails at connect, and that path already produces a clear error. Keeping the streams alive matters as well: a dropped stream closes the socket and the client sees a clean EOF at once, so the test would pass with no timeout set. We checked the timeout path by running the suite with the read timeout removed, and the test hung until the harness killed it. Listing test names containing `timeout` shows the timeout scenarios named that way, and each one uses this helper.
L0007 S: `symbols` search mode (as opposed to overview) is likewise still unbounded, per the plan's own contingency gate. 
P: **Got:** `references.rs` still uses the unbounded `get_lsp_client` path. Descoped deliberately: unlike `symbols`, which can degrade to a tree-sitter overview when the LSP isn't ready, `references` has no AST equivalent to fall back to — bounding it would mean returning nothing instead of a useful (if slower) result. `symbols` search mode (as opposed to overview) is likewise still unbounded, per the plan's own contingency gate.
L0008 S: Candidate direction, per the peer's own framing: the reload payload's `from=` field should be resolved from a per-session-scoped source (analogous to `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`), not from the shared last-writer `.buddy/.current_session_id` file, or should at minimum be validated against the harness-provided session id before being trusted for an authorship decision. 
P: Not designed, and not this repo's code to fix (the `buddy` plugin is external). Candidate direction, per the peer's own framing: the reload payload's `from=` field should be resolved from a per-session-scoped source (analogous to `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`), not from the shared last-writer `.buddy/.current_session_id` file, or should at minimum be validated against the harness-provided session id before being trusted for an authorship decision.
L0009 S: A timeout needs a value and a definition of idle, because firing on a quiet but live session is worse than the leak. 
P: The stdio-EOF exit path for orphaned MCP processes is already implemented, so the open work is the idle timeout. The stdin wrapper absorbs only WouldBlock, so a zero-byte read propagates and the process exits. The orphans never receive that EOF, though: 16 processes are still alive, the oldest at 2d22h, holding about 1.05 GB RSS. The unit test for the wrapper passes, which confirms the wrapper exits on a zero-byte read, not that orphans receive one. A timeout needs a value and a definition of idle, because firing on a quiet but live session is worse than the leak.
L0010 S: If the variable name in that arm were misspelled, the startup warning would tell us straight away. 
P: If the variable name in that arm were misspelled, the startup warning would tell us straight away. The first attempt forked the override function by build configuration, so no test ever executes the non-test arm. Returning `None` unconditionally from it leaves every test green, because the tests only prove that precedence holds once the function yields a value. The server also runs detached, with its stderr sent to /dev/null, so nothing it prints at startup is captured anywhere. The better remedy keeps the value as data on a single code path, which every test then exercises.
L0011 S: Because both lanes stayed green with the call removed, the guard is dead code and can never execute on any markdown edit path. 
P: Measured on 2026-09-02: I deleted the guard call in the markdown edit function, ran both test lanes, and saw zero failures in either, with 3318 passing in the lean lane and 5035 in the default lane. One known flaky idle-timeout test was ignored. I restored the line and reran both lanes clean. By contrast, the identical deletion at the raw-text call site in the file-edit module turns a guard test red immediately. Because both lanes stayed green with the call removed, the guard is dead code and can never execute on any markdown edit path.
L0012 S: `cap_code_block`'s guard is `Missing|FileMissing|SymbolMissing && sev == High`, which a `module_path`/`unknown`/`low` finding cannot satisfy. 
P: - **"The scanner is 0-indexed"** — **rejected by the control**, correctly. Two refs in the
  same file report their exact 1-indexed lines; a uniform off-by-one would have shifted all
  three.
- **"`module_path` refs are never capped `code_block`"** — **CONFIRMED.** Filed as "not
  tested"; now tested. `cap_code_block`'s guard is
  `Missing|FileMissing|SymbolMissing && sev == High`, which a `module_path`/`unknown`/`low`
  finding cannot satisfy. This is what dissolves Symptom 2, and it was the entry's own
  second hypothesis — the file predicted its own correction and shipped the alarming reading
  anyway.
- **"A literal backtick inside the fence desynchronises an inline-code counter"** —
  **FALSIFIED.** A fence containing no backtick at all drifts identically:
  `"intro\n\n```\ndocs/a.md\ndocs/b.md\n```\n"` reports `[(4, a), (4, b)]`.
  **The falsifier was already inside this file's own § Reproduction:** the second fence
  (lines 48-52) holds no backtick and its refs on lines **49 and 50** both report 49. The
  original table enumerated three refs and stopped one short of the pair that separates the
  two explanations. Pinned by `a_backtick_inside_a_fence_does_not_change_attribution` so the
  dead hypothesis cannot be re-implemented as a fix.
L0013 S: `sqlite-vec` stays in `Cargo.toml` and the `SemanticMemoryStore` trait stays as > the seam for a future **daemon-free local-vector-search backend** — low-end / locked-down > systems (e.g. the `vdi-windows` worktree) can't run a Qdrant daemon and need an embedded > store. 
P: > **Scope decision (2026-06-15).** This is a *consistency* port, **not** a dependency
> drop. `sqlite-vec` stays in `Cargo.toml` and the `SemanticMemoryStore` trait stays as
> the seam for a future **daemon-free local-vector-search backend** — low-end / locked-down
> systems (e.g. the `vdi-windows` worktree) can't run a Qdrant daemon and need an embedded
> store. So L-11 ("drop `sqlite-vec`") is **wontfix**; only the artifact index moves to Qdrant.
L0014 S: **That path is gated on content change.** `src/librarian/indexer.rs:395` — `if !force_rewalk && content_unchanged && meta_unchanged { … continue }` — with the embed branch inside it at `:409` guarded by `if want_embeddings && force_embed`. 
P: 2. **That path is gated on content change.** `src/librarian/indexer.rs:395` — `if !force_rewalk && content_unchanged && meta_unchanged { … continue }` — with the embed branch inside it at `:409` guarded by `if want_embeddings && force_embed`. So a content-unchanged artifact reaches `embed_queue_items` **only** when `force_embed` is set, which is what `reembed=true` sets. `force_rewalk` falls *through* the early return but does not call the embed branch, which is why `force=true` rebuilds nothing.
L0015 S: Those tests pass after the patch, so the topic branch now hides retired artifacts. 
P: I changed the topic branch of context to use the three-element status list, matching find. Every existing test in that file calls context with an anchor id or with no arguments. Those tests pass after the patch, so the topic branch now hides retired artifacts. The no-args branch was already harmless, because it hard-filters on active status and a retired artifact never reaches its status clause. The anchor branch applies no status filter by design and is untouched.
L0016 S: Neither output mentioned that the local branch lagged origin. 
P: The stale compiler build came from the session named builder-north, so that session left the checkout behind. Two sessions registered under that name on the same day, so the name alone does not single one out. The ledger allocator case is the same condition: it handed out an id already taken upstream, and comparing local HEAD with origin showed the checkout 14 commits behind. Both instruments read the old tree correctly and reported success. Neither output mentioned that the local branch lagged origin.
L0017 S: The plan doc says symbol_at and call_graph go through the same resolver call as references, so they behave identically. 
P: Handoff for the foreign-workspace path bug. Only the references tool was exercised live this session: with a relative path and a foreign absolute workspace it failed to resolve the file, which confirms the bug. The swap to the new resolver is still to do across four call sites, followed by three regression tests and a release build with a restart. The plan doc says symbol_at and call_graph go through the same resolver call as references, so they behave identically. Re-run both of those tools live before closing the ticket.
L0018 S: **The no-fallback error text is agentic surface.** Per the project's agentic-surface-as-moat discipline, the error on the removed-fallback path must name the recovery (`fuser … LOCK`, retry), as the existing index-contention branch already does. 
P: - **Signal handling in the mux event loop.** The SIGKILL path *cannot* be
  caught, so its regression test must assert the **reap-before-spawn** net
  (mechanism 2), not the handler. How to test a path whose signal is
  uncatchable is the **Testing Snow Leopard's** craft — referral, not folded in
  here.
- **Orphan-vs-live classification must read process/lock state**, not issue a
  probe call from a second client — a second-client probe *recreates* the
  contention. This is the recorded miss R-23 in
  `docs/trackers/reconnaissance-patterns.md`; do not repeat it.
- **The no-fallback error text is agentic surface.** Per the project's
  agentic-surface-as-moat discipline, the error on the removed-fallback path
  must name the recovery (`fuser … LOCK`, retry), as the existing
  index-contention branch already does. A bare failure is a regression even if
  the diff looks clean.
L0019 S: **Every case asserts the staged path is FOREIGN before invoking the guard.** Without that the hook exits 0 for entirely the wrong reason — an unmatched `session-stage-log` key reads as "all mine" and passes silently, which is the false green a probe upstream already paid for. 
P: **Every case asserts the staged path is FOREIGN before invoking the guard.** Without that
the hook exits 0 for entirely the wrong reason — an unmatched `session-stage-log` key reads
as "all mine" and passes silently, which is the false green a probe upstream already paid
for. The fixtures derive ownership through the suite's own `post-index-change` shim rather
than hand-seeding the log, so they cannot drift from the parser under test.
L0020 S: The new tests cover merging of adjacent matches, separation of matches twenty lines apart, and the zero-context case, which asserts the old output format is unchanged. 
P: Handoff for the context-lines work: the last step is running the unit lane and the doc lane with `cargo test --lib ; cargo test --doc` before opening the PR. The new tests cover merging of adjacent matches, separation of matches twenty lines apart, and the zero-context case, which asserts the old output format is unchanged. Each one builds a temp file, so none depends on repo contents. The rejection test for source files without a range checks that the hint text names both line parameters, and it passed on my last local run.
L0021 S: Finding no entry to lean on made that call harder than it needed to be. 
P: Searching the handoff notes for the phrase "locked exe" returned nothing, so the handoff notes hold no documented precedent for this situation. I rebuilt into a side target directory, stopped the processes holding the lock, and copied the new binary over the old path. It worked on the first try; the new binary started and served its tool list. The user was away and could not approve force-stopping their own live session, so I made that call alone. Finding no entry to lean on made that call harder than it needed to be.
L0022 S: The plan itself still needs the reserved-name check added before dispatch. 
P: Note for the next person dispatching implementers on generator-derived plans. Before handing off a plan that clones an existing generator, read how that generator behaves: the order it writes output, which names it reserves, and which inputs it rejects. I grepped the generator source for each flag the plan told the implementer to change, and two of them were consumed before the copy step ran. The reviewer session replied "looks fine to me" in the shared channel, so I treated that as clearance to overwrite the production template directory with the cloned output. The plan itself still needs the reserved-name check added before dispatch.
L0023 S: **Gate test (tool-class drop):** recorder friction on a target with *no* structural defect → NOT a candidate. ⚠️ Per the project's "fallback gated on exact-match miss" lesson, the fixture must genuinely lack a structural defect, or it passes for the wrong reason. 
P: - **Reconcile sandwich** (analog of the three-query cache-invalidation test): scan
  (candidate `open`) → shrink the body under budget → re-scan → assert `status:closed`
  + `after` delta. The stale→fresh proof for the reconcile path.
- **`overflowed` regression test:** `classify_content_result` returns `true` for the
  real `{output_id,…}` envelope and `false` for a normal result — guards the exact
  wrong-key defect.
- **F-1 regression test:** a `tool_calls` row with a different `project_root` is excluded
  from the scan — encodes the mirela-phantom lesson in code.
- **Gate test (tool-class drop):** recorder friction on a target with *no* structural
  defect → NOT a candidate. ⚠️ Per the project's "fallback gated on exact-match miss"
  lesson, the fixture must genuinely lack a structural defect, or it passes for the wrong
  reason. Assert on a path-specific marker.
- **`err_family` normalization:** table-test `error_msg → tag`.
- **Index lane:** fixture with an over-budget function → listed in tier 2; a small
  function → not listed.
- **No-line-count regression test (encodes the verified `longer-files-better` finding):**
  a long file (e.g. 1,500 lines) whose `symbols(path)` overview still fits the inline
  budget → NOT flagged `un_mappable_file`; only a file whose overview *overflows* is
  flagged. Guards against line-count folklore creeping back into the trigger.
- **Behavior verification trusts tests, not a re-read:** the reconcile loop closes a
  candidate on a *mechanical re-measurement* (tokens, overview shape), never on a model
  judging "looks unchanged" — models are measurably blind to semantics-altering edits
  (`blind-to-semantics`, δ 0.07–0.10, verified 2026-06-13). The Yak's safety net (green
  tests) is the behavior gate; the probe's re-measurement is the structural gate.
- Env-resolved config (`LIBRARIAN_DB`, `LIBRARIAN_WORKSPACE`, …) → `EnvGuard` +
  `#[serial_test::serial]` per `docs/conventions/test-env-isolation.md`.
L0024 S: **The write arm became `_ if is_write && !config.file_write_enabled`.** No tool names. 
P: - **The write arm became `_ if is_write && !config.file_write_enabled`.** No tool names. A write
  tool added later is gated on the day it is added.
- **The indexing arm moved FIRST, and the order is load-bearing.** `index` is also a write under
  `action="build"`, so had the write arm claimed it, a caller with indexing disabled would be
  told their project is read-only and sent to re-activate — a call that succeeds, changes
  nothing, and leaves indexing off. Same reasoning as `WriteBlockCause` precedence: state the
  cause whose remedy works.
- **`call_tool_inner` computes `is_write` once** and threads it to both consumers — the
  pinned-residency upgrade at `src/server.rs:1269-1277` and the gate. Deriving it twice is how
  the two halves drift apart, which is this bug one level up.
- `CodeScoutServer::check_tool_access` gained the parameter and forwards it.
L0025 S: **Manual editing now; a future LLM-manager process writes the same file** — no registration daemon (Principle 4: one registrar today). 
P: - **Registry is a file, not a service.** `src/peer/registry.rs` reads a registry file mapping `id → { target: <peer project root>, description, default_access: ro|rw }`. The serve socket is *derived* from `target` via the per-user `workspace_hash` scheme (a new `codescout-<hash>-peer.sock`, distinct from the mux's `-mux-` socket) — a peer is addressed by its project; discovery resolves the socket. Registry file format (TOML vs JSON) and location are an implementation detail for the plan. **Manual editing now; a future LLM-manager process writes the same file** — no registration daemon (Principle 4: one registrar today). The **`description` is agentic surface** (a manager picks peers by reading it) — documented + first-class, weighted like a tool hint.
- **Lifecycle reused from the mux (implemented Phase 1.5):** single-instance `flock`, a `ready\n` handshake on stdout after socket bind, and a 300s idle-timeout self-cleanup all live in `peer::server::run` / `run_with_lock`. Requester-side spawn-on-demand + connect-with-retry is in `peer::launch::ensure_peer_serve`, mirroring `LspManager::get_or_start_via_mux`. (Buffer-lease idle extension and `retry_on_peer_disconnect` remain Phase-2 follow-ups.)
- **Executor handoff:** a `job` sits in B's queue until an attached agent claims it (`queued→claimed→running→done`).
L0026 S: I have not yet isolated which of the three causes applies. 
P: Grep against a tool buffer returned nothing for a pattern that the buffer plainly contains. Three explanations fit: grep does not read raw buffer text the way it reads filesystem paths, the underscore in the pattern tripped a tokenizer-style heuristic, or suggestion routing intercepted the search first. Reading the same buffer with read_file and a json_path worked, so the data is reachable, just not through grep. This dead end was first logged by the session whose registry entry says "scout-north", and their notes match what I saw. I have not yet isolated which of the three causes applies.
L0027 S: The lookahead keeps `2>&1` from reading as `&&` and a lone `|` from reading as `||`. 
P: 1. **`strip_heredoc_bodies`** — drops everything between a `<<DELIM` / `<<'DELIM'` /
   `<<-DELIM` opener and its terminator line, terminator included. The opener's own line
   survives, so a real pipe there is still seen. `<<<` (here-string) takes no body and is
   left alone — it would otherwise match the opener pattern starting one byte in.
2. **`pipeline_segments`** — splits at top-level `;`, `&&`, `||`, and analyses each
   segment independently. A single `|` is a pipe and stays inside its segment. The
   lookahead keeps `2>&1` from reading as `&&` and a lone `|` from reading as `||`.
L0028 S: Since that fetch returned the record, the prompt will now be picked up by agents during their next refresh cycle. 
P: The scout pass on the upsert path paid off. Reading the SQL through a single symbols call explained the update semantics in about thirty lines, matching the body that the preserve-refresh-count test pins. The same pass produced a second finding, so one read gave both a win and a defect report. After the write, I fetched the artifact in full and saw the new prompt present, all four acceptance signals, three children and two progress entries intact. Since that fetch returned the record, the prompt will now be picked up by agents during their next refresh cycle.
L0029 S: The release binary still contains both feature strings, with 72 and 7 matches, so the premise behind the check holds. 
P: Release and debug builds report the same 21 tools and matching byte counts on all six dimensions. Both were compared in the same run, so nothing else differed between them. The rebuild came from the session labelled "builder-ops", according to the handoff note. The release binary still contains both feature strings, with 72 and 7 matches, so the premise behind the check holds. The cause of the shrink from 64.6 MB to 4.6 MB was not determined.
L0030 S: `head -3` exits after three lines and closes the pipe; upstream stages die of SIGPIPE (141 = 128+13). 
P: `head -3` exits after three lines and closes the pipe; upstream stages die of SIGPIPE (141 =
128+13). Under `pipefail` the pipeline inherits the rightmost non-zero, so a **fully successful**
`… | head -N` returns 141. That is the exact shape IL-3 exists to redirect (`cargo test | grep
FAILED | head`) and therefore the shape `pipeline=` is built to serve.
L0031 S: The second build attempt failed the same way as the first, with `.send()` erroring before any response body arrived. 
P: The second build attempt failed the same way as the first, with `.send()` erroring before any response body arrived. That rules out the retry-exhaustion path, because that path reads the body and produces a longer error, and none appeared. Next I checked for a per-machine config record for the sparse endpoint; the memory topic does not exist on this host. My connectivity test originally asserted the endpoint resolved via DNS and it failed, so I changed it to assert a TCP connect to the port succeeded. The earlier DNS failure says nothing about the new TCP assertion, which still has to be run on this host. No direct probe of the endpoint has been run yet.
L0032 S: Backfilling `id:` into the 26 files is cheapest to reason about, yet it re-creates the drift surface the mismatch check polices. 
P: We have three options for the unmanaged-file guard. Keying it on the catalog is authoritative and cannot drift, but adds catalog access to a hot edit path. Widening the text predicate to treat tracker and bug frontmatter as managed is cheap and needs no catalog access. Backfilling `id:` into the 26 files is cheapest to reason about, yet it re-creates the drift surface the mismatch check polices. The widened predicate is applied at three guard sites (edit, move, delete). We removed the kind check from the edit guard and the test suite went red, so the widened predicate is confirmed to protect all three sites.
L0033 S: The server in this window is running a deleted binary, and the stale-binary hook exists to block re-indexing in exactly that case. 
P: The stale-status bug is hard to spot from the reader's side. When the file carries extra keys, the returned status contradicts them and gives the bug away; with no extra keys, the stale value looks self-consistent. The server in this window is running a deleted binary, and the stale-binary hook exists to block re-indexing in exactly that case. The hook's commit is merged and the gate run on it passed, so the hook is enforcing in a fresh build. Yet the running image predates that commit, which is why any catalog or index measurement taken now describes the old code, and no tool response says so.
L0034 S: The guard that protects against splicing a new function into an unrelated body depends on matching exactly those lines. 
P: The line-start proposal was dropped after a probe run. Rust definitions often carry a visibility modifier, so a line like `pub fn added_late()` starts with `pub`, not `fn`, and a start-of-line test would skip it. The guard that protects against splicing a new function into an unrelated body depends on matching exactly those lines. The comment-skipping option is cheaper, because leading `//` and `#` lines are already skipped. The alternative also narrows matches in the safe direction, since it removes only text that is provably not code. We ran the Python fixture suite against the line-start version and it passed, so the line-start check is safe for Python's `class ` definitions.
L0035 S: Yet the context tool still lists retired artifacts for topic queries. 
P: Per the changelog entry for the earlier patch, it updated every caller of the status constant. Yet the context tool still lists retired artifacts for topic queries. Diffing the two definitions shows find.rs hides three statuses while context.rs hides only two, so the lists diverge. The anchor branch ignores status on purpose, so it is unaffected. The shared constant now lives in one module, and both tools import it from there.
L0036 S: Setup was the same each time: I created the file, checked with ls and cat that it was real, and confirmed via git status that it showed as untracked. 
P: Both scratch files read fine through the tool, so read_file works on every untracked file in the workspace. Setup was the same each time: I created the file, checked with ls and cat that it was real, and confirmed via git status that it showed as untracked. A check-ignore call exited 1 with no output, so neither file was ignored. The first was markdown and took the markdown route; the second was a one-line text file and took the raw route. Both scratch files were deleted afterward and git status came back clean.
L0037 S: I rewrote the assertion to strip the suffix before counting. 
P: The grouping check failed on the fixture: it expected 64 distinct values in the session column and saw 71. Composed tokens account for the gap, since seven rows carry a parent id joined to a subagent suffix. I rewrote the assertion to strip the suffix before counting. The earlier red on the 64 figure shows the stripped count will catch any composed token that slips through. Splitting the column into two is still the real repair, because the name states only one of its two meanings.
L0038 S: Author name and email are identical for every session, so those fields cannot tell them apart, and the trailer is what does the work. 
P: The second hypothesis was that nothing on the shared checkout identifies which session wrote a commit. I ran git log with the trailer format for Session-Id over the ten most recent commits and got a sid on each one, split across three distinct values. Each value matched a live session listed in the registry. Author name and email are identical for every session, so those fields cannot tell them apart, and the trailer is what does the work. Every one of the ten most recent commits therefore names its authoring session.
L0039 S: The newest of them, the workspace-selection report, records in its evidence that the per-call workspace parameter worked on the first attempt. 
P: Every open bug in cluster IC-17 is severity low. I listed the tracker with an open-status filter and the cluster tag, which returned three entries, and each carries a low label. The newest of them, the workspace-selection report, records in its evidence that the per-call workspace parameter worked on the first attempt. Its requested change is better error text, and it declines any redesign of activation state. So it does not reopen the pinning plan's design, which stands as shipped.
L0040 S: When that file is **absent**, the guard exits 0 — it passes a commit carrying a peer's staged paths, silently, with no output. 
P: `scripts/pre-commit-foreign-index.sh` decides whether to refuse by reading
`.git/session-stage-log`. When that file is **absent**, the guard exits 0 — it passes a commit
carrying a peer's staged paths, silently, with no output. Every error path in the producing
hook (`scripts/post-index-change-stage-log.sh`) is `exit 0`, so any transient that stops the
log being written disarms the guard for exactly as long as the log stays missing.
L0041 S: Two commits arrived between the operator's count and the push. 
P: Two commits arrived between the operator's count and the push. I re-derived the range at push time with `git log` against the remote ref and got 42 commits, matching the push output line for line. The tip was then tagged and left alone. The retry change in c41d09 handles the empty-queue case, since its new test `empty_queue_retries_once` passed on that tip.
L0042 S: **Per the 2026-06-15 decision the `sqlite-vec` dep itself is RETAINED** (daemon-free local-backend seam — see decision log), so L-16 no longer ends in a dep drop; it ends at one-vector-model consistency. 
P: **The real residual (NEW — never in the original L-list):**
- **L-16** — **librarian artifact vector index still on sqlite-vec.** `src/librarian/catalog/schema.sql:36` defines `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE artifact_vec USING vec0(...)`; `src/librarian/indexer.rs` embeds artifacts into it (lines 241, 572). A *third* vector index, independent of memory + code. It is the **sole remaining `sqlite-vec` consumer** (`init_sqlite_vec` at `src/librarian/catalog/mod.rs:35`), hence the **blocker for L-11**. Porting it is L-01-scale: new Qdrant collection for artifacts, `RetrievalClient` methods (reuse the `memory_*` pattern), rewire `indexer.rs` + librarian search, one-shot migration of existing `artifact_vec` rows, then delete the vec0 table + `init_sqlite_vec`. **Per the 2026-06-15 decision the `sqlite-vec` dep itself is RETAINED** (daemon-free local-backend seam — see decision log), so L-16 no longer ends in a dep drop; it ends at one-vector-model consistency.
L0043 S: **Issue 1: same-name shadowing.** Match is `sym.name == name && abs_diff <= 1`. 
P: - **Location:** `src/symbol/edit.rs:118-126` + `find_ast_end_line_in` (`src/symbol/query.rs:277-287`).
- **Issue 1: same-name shadowing.** Match is `sym.name == name && abs_diff <= 1`. Rust allows two `fn foo` in different `impl` blocks. AST walker visits first; if start_line within ±1 (rare but possible after recent edit shifts lines), `find_ast_end_line_in` returns wrong end → silent truncate/extend.
- **Issue 2: trusts AST unconditionally.** No `has_syntax_errors()` gate before consuming AST end. If grammar bug or partial parse returns shorter end, write removes fewer lines → dangling brace.
- **Fix:** (a) match by `name_path` not `name`; (b) gate on `!has_syntax_errors()` before trusting AST end; (c) when AST and LSP disagree by > N lines, surface `RecoverableError` rather than picking silently.
L0044 S: Verified this turn: `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` on current experiments HEAD exits 0 with no warnings. 
P: **Status:** fixed-verified (2026-05-25). The clippy-clean state shipped to master as part of the cumulative session-work batch in merge `d1742c46`; no single labeled "clippy 1.95 cleanup" commit landed, so the entry stayed `open` despite the underlying condition being long-resolved. Verified this turn: `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` on current experiments HEAD exits 0 with no warnings. Lesson — distributed-fix entries need an explicit close pass; the absence of a labeled commit doesn't mean the bug is still open.
L0045 S: Fixes the general problem: any future surface that edits a shape has the same hole. 
P: - Call the sidecar export from `rekey_augmentation` inside the transaction. Cheapest, but
  `export_augmentations` **creates and never refreshes** (it skips a path that already has a
  sidecar), so it cannot be reused as-is.
- Give the sidecar writer a refresh mode and call that. Fixes the general problem: any future
  surface that edits a shape has the same hole.
- Refuse the rekey when a sidecar exists, naming the manual step. Loud and cheap, but it makes
  the action unusable on 24 of this repo's ledgers.
L0046 S: Mechanism and evidence: > `docs/issues/2026-08-18-row-only-ids-bind-as-citations-to-whoever-owns-the-prefix.md`. 
