L0068 S: Every caller in the catalog find module goes from AST to SQL. 
P: The filter AST type is reusable, but the only engine compiles it into a SQL WHERE clause. Two things block reuse at entry grain. First, the field allowlist is hardcoded to artifact columns, and the leaf compiler rejects anything outside it, so entry fields like category or severity cannot pass. Second, operator semantics live in SQL, so they would need a Rust reimplementation for an in-memory path. Every caller in the catalog find module goes from AST to SQL. From the source alone, the compile function appears to reject an unknown field, but the return value on that path was not run.
L0069 S: What remains is the hint size, which is a separate bug and has no ticket yet. 
P: The YAML hint builder and the TOML hint builder both join an unbounded collect, so neither can drop items silently. I confirmed this by reading each call site: no take sits before the join in either one. What remains is the hint size, which is a separate bug and has no ticket yet. The two-ended window landed in the earlier change, and its tests pass on the current tree. Session 3e9b41d7 closed the TOML question after reading both call sites.
L0070 S: If a future table-copy migration is added, apply the same foreign_keys-off discipline. 
P: Fixed. Note: catalogs already migrated to v6 before this fix lost their
pre-migration augmentations/events from the live DB — but the loss is
**recoverable from the `catalog.db.pre-v6-bak.<ts>` snapshots** the migration
writes before running (see ## Recovery for the runbook; 7 trackers restored
2026-07-05). This fix protects catalogs migrating v(<6)→v6 from here on. If a future table-copy migration is
added, apply the same foreign_keys-off discipline.
L0071 S: Both were red on the broken build and green after the patch, so the assertion targets the regression that happened. 
P: The banner regression put a raw action attribute in text meant for the model, and the test has to catch that exact shape. Pinning the full sentence would fail on every rewording, so we assert two properties instead: the banner does not contain `action="`, and it does name the refresh flag. Both were red on the broken build and green after the patch, so the assertion targets the regression that happened. The snapshot renderer also produced a byte-identical banner on the second run, so the banner text is confirmed to steer the agent toward the refresh flag in live sessions.
L0072 S: The only recorded trigger is one project switching its embedder after indexing. 
P: The vector table for artifacts is created with a fixed 768-wide embedding column, and vec0 has no per-row dimension, so the width is decided once at creation. Rows are keyed by a hash of the absolute path with no project or model prefix. Before this change, the write path bailed on any dimension mismatch. The only recorded trigger is one project switching its embedder after indexing. That bail is our protection against mixed-dimension rows, since the indexer runs as a detached background job with its log output discarded and nothing polls its exit status. Any new migration path has to keep that in mind.
L0073 S: That is why the caller-side comment kept teaching the rejected argument. 
P: The comment above the cast calls it safe, but the history says otherwise. Session 5a20f7c3 made the commit that replaced the cast with a helper that refuses invalid pids. Its in-code note says the cast would turn a single-process signal into a group-wide one. The commit's file list covers the platform module and the rendezvous tool but not the LSP client. That is why the caller-side comment kept teaching the rejected argument.
L0074 S: `guard "$B"` then reaches `scripts/pre-commit-foreign-index.sh:153`'s `[ -s "$log" ] || exit 0` and exits silently → failures 2 and 3 (`missing: EXIT=1`, and no `del.txt` in the refusal). 
P: 1. The case (`tests/hooks-discrimination.sh:187`) runs `rm -f .git/session-stage-log`, then
   `git status` as peer B, **expecting `post-index-change` to recreate the log**.
2. `post-index-change` fires on index **writes** (`man githooks`, quoted at
   `scripts/post-index-change-stage-log.sh:26`). `git status` rewrites the index only when it
   has stat information to refresh — sometimes it has none. No write, no hook, no log.
3. `owner_of()` (`tests/hooks-discrimination.sh:100`) awks that path with **no existence
   guard**, so it fatals and yields `''` where the case wants `-`. → failure 1.
4. `guard "$B"` then reaches `scripts/pre-commit-foreign-index.sh:153`'s
   `[ -s "$log" ] || exit 0` and exits silently → failures 2 and 3 (`missing: EXIT=1`, and no
   `del.txt` in the refusal).
L0075 S: Run staging commands with the cwd inside the repo (`cd "$P" && git add f`), or use the joined `--git-dir=`/`--work-tree=` form, which parses correctly. 
P: Mutation discipline per CLAUDE.md § *Testing Discipline* — the guarded **site** here is the
invocation form, and the pre-existing suite sampled exactly one of them, which is why 21 cases
passed against this defect for the whole of its life.
Run staging commands with the cwd inside the repo (`cd "$P" && git add f`), or use the joined
`--git-dir=`/`--work-tree=` form, which parses correctly.
L0076 S: It is not a requirement: `run_command` exposes no per-stage control surface, the > MCP call is request/response with no mid-flight channel, #2 makes pipeline mutually > exclusive with `run_in_background` (closing the one handle-based path), and the existing > cancellation intent is explicitly the opposite — `src/tools/run_command/inner.rs:436-439` > kills *"the entire pipeline … not just the shell"* on future-drop, by design. 
P: > **Note on why per-stage cancellation is NOT among C's costs.** An earlier framing of this
> tracker's open questions treated "kill one stage, keep the pipeline" as C's deciding
> drawback. It is not a requirement: `run_command` exposes no per-stage control surface, the
> MCP call is request/response with no mid-flight channel, #2 makes pipeline mutually
> exclusive with `run_in_background` (closing the one handle-based path), and the existing
> cancellation intent is explicitly the opposite — `src/tools/run_command/inner.rs:436-439`
> kills *"the entire pipeline … not just the shell"* on future-drop, by design. The
> SIGPIPE-driven behaviours callers actually rely on (`head` closing stdin kills the producer;
> killing a producer EOFs its consumers) are free in any shell pipeline and survive under C —
> `unix.rs:80` resets SIGPIPE to `SIG_DFL` in `pre_exec` precisely so they work. See
> `design-backlog-session-log:F-4`.
L0077 S: The refusal is emitted from three places: the commit hook, the push gate, and the status wrapper, each carrying its own copy of the owner check. 
P: A third session hit the refusal and misread the two-name list, which cost a live reader four minutes. The refusal is emitted from three places: the commit hook, the push gate, and the status wrapper, each carrying its own copy of the owner check. I blanked the owner list in the commit hook only, and the mutation run turned red with two failing tests. Both tests assert that the message names the file holder, and both failed on the blanked list. Because that mutant was caught, the owner check is verified at the commit hook, the one site I mutated. The push gate and the status wrapper still have no test that reads their owner list.
L0078 S: **Fix:** Add `// SAFETY:` comment pinning allowlist semantics, OR assert `stem.contains('/') == false && stem != ".."`. 
P: - **Location:** `src/mcp_resources/memory.rs:33-39` (`MemoryProvider::lookup`).
- **Evidence:** Strips `memory://` prefix, matches against pre-enumerated `entries()` set (paths from `read_dir(self.dir)` filtered to `.md`). `memory://../../etc/passwd` cannot resolve outside `self.dir` — lookup misses. **Safe by allowlist.** Risk if anyone "optimizes" to `self.dir.join(stem + ".md")`.
- **Fix:** Add `// SAFETY:` comment pinning allowlist semantics, OR assert `stem.contains('/') == false && stem != ".."`.
- **Confidence:** high (current code safe; comment is preventative).
L0079 S: Root-causing those is unbounded wine-quirk work with low payoff (they are not real Windows bugs, per the MSVC baseline); the doctor test is the only one worth chasing as a real defect. 
P: **Remaining: 12 skips (down from 20).** These are NOT fixed here and stay
skip-listed — they are a different, wine-emulation-specific class (the symbols
