L0080 S: **The block is gated off.** `:309` is `if [ -n "$ack" ] && [ "$ack_matched" != "all" ]`, and `acked()` sets `ack_matched="all"` on the wildcard (`:98`). 
P: 1. **The block is gated off.** `:309` is `if [ -n "$ack" ] && [ "$ack_matched" != "all" ]`,
   and `acked()` sets `ack_matched="all"` on the wildcard (`:98`).
2. **The data it would print was never collected.** `acked()`'s wildcard arm is
   `[ "$_a" = "all" ] && { ack_matched="all"; return 0; }` — it returns at `:98` and
   **never reaches `:103`**, where per-sid accumulation happens. So `ack_matched` holds the
   literal string `all` and no sids. Removing the gate would reach a branch with nothing to
   name.
L0081 S: The harness itself runs its two lanes as `cargo test --test brief_shape && cargo test --test brief_limits`. 
P: The research agent brief limits it to one follow-up query, and step five states this plainly, so a thin first result cannot trigger a retry loop. Sources that fail the invalidation filters get dropped before synthesis, which keeps the output short. In the eval harness I checked 20 transcripts and none contained a second follow-up call. The harness itself runs its two lanes as `cargo test --test brief_shape && cargo test --test brief_limits`.
L0082 S: The dynamic-embed module sits behind a single feature flag. 
P: The dynamic-embed module sits behind a single feature flag. The only CI job that turns the flag on runs a type-check across all targets, and no lane runs tests with it enabled. That job's log ends in the check step and never launches a test binary, so a green result there shows the file compiles and nothing more. Because that job passed on the PR, the embedder path was exercised before merge.
L0083 S: Work happens on the experiments branch because master is protected, and the branch name must be checked right before each commit, since concurrent sessions have switched the checkout twice while this plan was drafted. 
P: These are the ground conventions for the retrieval tasks. Work happens on the experiments branch because master is protected, and the branch name must be checked right before each commit, since concurrent sessions have switched the checkout twice while this plan was drafted. Every task touches retrieval, so the gate runs both the default and the server-stack configurations, which has its own CI lane. Worktree fixtures are built inside each test and never assumed from the host. The new path-filter test is confirmed to catch the exclusion defect, because it failed on the branch after we reintroduced the defect.
L0084 S: This change reads exactly two files, config/base.toml and config/local.toml, and both parsed without errors when I ran the loader. 
P: This change reads exactly two files, config/base.toml and config/local.toml, and both parsed without errors when I ran the loader.
L0085 S: All 14 input files compiled cleanly before any edit, which I checked with a build over the inputs directory. 
P: Round 1 of the eval ran all 14 cases end to end, and each produced one of the six verdicts. All 14 input files compiled cleanly before any edit, which I checked with a build over the inputs directory. The shared eval_common module now serves both nav_eval and edit_eval, and the round-1 verdict file is committed. H1 through H3 pass with R-02 exempt. The R-02 exemption note in the TODO file was written by Ines, since her commits landed in nav_eval around the same time.
L0086 S: `CODESCOUT_EMBEDDER_MODEL_NAME` stays **on top**, per the 2026-09-17 ruling. 
P: `CODESCOUT_EMBEDDER_MODEL_NAME` stays **on top**, per the 2026-09-17 ruling. Every stack
deployment sets it while leaving `[embeddings].model` at the built-in default, so letting
`model` win would have silently repointed all of them at `AllMiniLML6V2Q`. The shadowing
WARNING that makes the override visible is deliberately *not* here: `RetrievalConfig`
cannot yet distinguish a chosen `model` from a defaulted one, so a warning keyed on
difference alone would fire on every deployment that exists. That provenance is Task 5 of
`docs/plans/2026-09-17-embedding-config-consolidation.md`.
L0087 S: We chose to accept the re-orientation after a client sits idle past its TTL, rather than prevent it. 
P: We chose to accept the re-orientation after a client sits idle past its TTL, rather than prevent it. The governing invariant is to degrade to re-sending, never to suppressing, and a stale client has probably lost the original orientation anyway. Re-sending costs tokens once, whereas suppression fails silently. We rejected keeping a placeholder entry, because is_empty() would then return false for the rest of the session and the opener would never be sent again. That leaves the case where the map empties and the file is already gone. The docs for persist say it returns an error when the map is empty and the file is absent, so that path is safe.
L0088 S: **patch-id** — `90d4b2215b3ee26745e5d50de24a8ee3c1f86056` (`git show 9433feb3 > f && git patch-id --stable < f`; content hash of the diff, survives rebase **and** cherry-pick). 
P: - **SHA** — `9433feb3` (branch `experiments`; positional, dies on the next rebase)
- **patch-id** — `90d4b2215b3ee26745e5d50de24a8ee3c1f86056`
  (`git show 9433feb3 > f && git patch-id --stable < f`; content hash of the diff, survives
  rebase **and** cherry-pick). Single parent, 39474 bytes — neither merge-commit trap applies.
- **Gate** — fmt-mine 0 / clippy 0 / lean 0 / default 0 at tree `331d2c0f`;
  `tests/mutation-probe.sh` 21 passed, 0 failed.
L0089 S: Both alternatives are informative: a floor at zero means result bodies are too weak a channel, and a ceiling at one means the delegation line is unnecessary. 
P: The eval compares four arms on adoption of result-carried guidance and on resistance to forged in-content directives. P1 predicts ADOPTION(B) > ADOPTION(A), because the delegation line gives the envelope channel authority. Both alternatives are informative: a floor at zero means result bodies are too weak a channel, and a ceiling at one means the delegation line is unnecessary. For P2, the pre-registered risk is C < D, which would mean the delegation line opened an in-content hole, and in that case the wording does not ship. The harness runs each arm three times and writes scores to a results table that the analysis script reads on every run. If a forged directive is followed in arm C, a warning is printed to the harness's stderr, which is redirected to /dev/null in the batch runner, so that warning will catch any regression before ship.
L0090 S: The staleness check reads the declared date on each entry and compares it to the last commit touching that entry. 
P: The staleness check reads the declared date on each entry and compares it to the last commit touching that entry. It is wired into three consumers: the linter, the nightly CI report, and the index builder, each with its own comparison code. I planted one entry dated a year back and ran the linter, which flagged it with the expected code. Undeclared entries are not checked at all, because computing their date needs per-entry blame. One planted stale entry tripped the linter, so staleness detection is covered in the linter.
L0091 S: Check 4 scans a different directory, listed in the probe config, and its pattern has not been touched in this change. 
P: Check 3 now anchors the patch-id pattern to the end of the line and skips lines that begin with the git command, and its rerun over the ~40 archived files returns zero hits from the recovery recipe. Checks 1 through 4 each carry their own regex, so none of them share a predicate. I planted one stray match in the archive and check 3 reported it, so all four checks are known to report planted matches. Check 4 scans a different directory, listed in the probe config, and its pattern has not been touched in this change.
L0092 S: A reviewer with full database access would see the same rows but not that split, because the split lives only in the manifest. 
P: To rerun the corpus checks, use `cargo test --lib && cargo test --test corpus_prov`, one lane after the other. The provenance test reads the manifest and counts 412 synthetic rows against 96 real ones, so the real share is under a fifth of the data. A reviewer with full database access would see the same rows but not that split, because the split lives only in the manifest. That is why the manifest ships beside the data and why the test fails when the two disagree.
L0093 S: I opened the startup loader: it reads only the env-file variable or the .env in the global config directory, and its doc comment says it never looks at the working directory. 
P: A config edit meant for this host went into the gpu env file first and had no effect. I opened the startup loader: it reads only the env-file variable or the .env in the global config directory, and its doc comment says it never looks at the working directory. The edit had to be repeated in the amd file before the server saw it. The launcher script, which I opened, copies the amd file into that global directory at startup, so that file is the one to edit going forward.
L0094 S: Arm B scored 26 of 75 and Arm C scored 31 of 75, so rerank shows no measurable benefit on this run. 
P: Arm B scored 26 of 75 and Arm C scored 31 of 75, so rerank shows no measurable benefit on this run. Empty-result rates differ sharply across arms: 8 of 25 for no-sparse against 1 of 25 for lite, which means even the narrowest comparison is not fully clean. The empty-result harness bug was introduced by Tomás, since his commits were the ones landing near the buffering code that week.
L0095 S: According to the design doc, the checker also descends into Rust doc comments. 
P: The new link checker covers catalogued markdown only. I ran it over the docs tree and it flagged two of the four stale citations from the earlier audit, both in markdown files. According to the design doc, the checker also descends into Rust doc comments. The two Rust sites stay with the older audit tool, capped at medium severity by design. That cap is why the tracker entry reads gate-exists-but-capped rather than gate-exists.
L0096 S: The tracker linter warns on any entry missing that field, and its glob covers only the trackers/ directory. 
P: The reconnaissance ledger has 34 of 61 entries with no Status line, counted directly with grep on the file today. The tracker linter warns on any entry missing that field, and its glob covers only the trackers/ directory. The ledger sits under notes/. The linter's warning therefore keeps the ledger's Status lines in check. Nobody has pointed the linter at notes/, so the count above is the first measurement of that file in months.
L0097 S: Mixed-model runs number 16, and every foreign block is long, so the pattern matches manual model switches rather than automatic rerouting. 
P: The scan covered 746 session files and found 81 that contain the model under test. None recorded a refusal stop reason; the only stop reasons observed were tool use, end of turn, and stop sequence. Mixed-model runs number 16, and every foreign block is long, so the pattern matches manual model switches rather than automatic rerouting. The 11 longest mixed runs are attributed to the session named 'nightly-eval' in the registry. No run shows a short Opus interleave inside a stretch of the model under test.
L0098 S: The implementer's guard (`if !new_string.ends_with('\n') && edits.last().span.end == sec_end { push('\n') }`) faithfully reproduces legacy — verified across all cases. 
P: **Got (verified reality):** Legacy `perform_scoped_edit` did NOT splice the matched range — it rebuilt the whole section and ran `ensure_trailing_newline(&new_section)`. So when `old_string` consumed the section's trailing `\n` (the separator before the next heading) and `new_string` omitted it, legacy re-added the `\n`; the naive byte-splice would delete it and fuse the next heading onto the body. The implementer's guard (`if !new_string.ends_with('\n') && edits.last().span.end == sec_end { push('\n') }`) faithfully reproduces legacy — verified across all cases.
L0099 S: Trimming all of them would save roughly $0.0002 per request, so the cost axis barely moves. 
P: The tool schema carries 51 parameters with about 12,700 characters of descriptions, and no single one is a fat target. Trimming all of them would save roughly $0.0002 per request, so the cost axis barely moves. The open question is whether the prose steers behaviour. I planted a misleading sentence in the `path` description and the eval flagged it in every run, so the eval is sensitive to description edits on all 51 parameters. One measurement settled the cost question; the behaviour question still needs probes placed at more than one parameter.
L0100 S: The full verified synthesis is **craft-shaped** and lives in the Dzo's own heuristics (the global buddy skill), per the reconnaissance promotion routing — it is deliberately **not** duplicated into a codescout project memory. 
P: The full verified synthesis is **craft-shaped** and lives in the Dzo's own heuristics
(the global buddy skill), per the reconnaissance promotion routing — it is deliberately
**not** duplicated into a codescout project memory. This spec is its project-shaped
projection: the lens (codescout) decides which properties matter — naming amplified
(address + retrieval key + isolated-symbol comprehension), raw file length neutralized
(the map is the unit, the symbol is the body), graph structure carried by
`references`/`call_graph` not prose, types kept for the LSP.
