L0102 S: That is what was measured. 
P: The pre-registered falsifier needs only a pair: a writer that could list its peer and still collided. That is what was measured. Sessions 4e8a12bd and f03c9977 each appeared in the other's listing and exchanged eight messages before the overlapping edit. Both registered under the name "writer", since names are self-reported and unchecked. Session 4e8a12bd ignored the listing entry for its peer and edited the shared file anyway. The population claim in the ledger is not needed for this result.
L0103 S: That covers cross-process races, where separate server processes share one catalog file, because IMMEDIATE takes the write lock before the read; the in-process mutex only covers threads in a single process. 
P: The allocation design puts the read-max-write sequence inside one SQLite IMMEDIATE transaction on the augmentation params. That covers cross-process races, where separate server processes share one catalog file, because IMMEDIATE takes the write lock before the read; the in-process mutex only covers threads in a single process. A catalog-layer function performs the allocation and a thin MCP module exposes it through the artifact action dispatch. The duplicate-number collision in the spec was first logged by session c07d5e28, which we treat as the owner of that failure report. Two-process stress tests are the next step.
L0104 S: The formatter step also exited cleanly, so the new server code is formatted to the project's style. 
P: The gate came back green, and the run is attributable because `ad379a7c` committed the client file first, leaving the server file as the only modified Rust in the tree. All four lane counters read zero. Per-lane controls back up the counting: this test ran 1/1, the prompts lane ran 101/101, so the counting method works, and the librarian lane ran 0/1760, which shows that lane is vacuous for librarian and the 1 is a measurement. The formatter step also exited cleanly, so the new server code is formatted to the project's style.
