L0117 S: The stalled run that burned three extra round-trips belonged to the session with sessionId 7f3a91c2, which we can therefore blame for the wasted budget. 
P: Overflow handling was traced through the handoff logs this morning. When a result is too large, the agent gets a compact envelope with an output_id, a byte count, and a hint. For tools that ship a real compact formatter, that envelope often answers the question directly, as with grep returning the matching lines. For the rest, the envelope is metadata only, so the agent must call again before doing anything useful. The stalled run that burned three extra round-trips belonged to the session with sessionId 7f3a91c2, which we can therefore blame for the wasted budget. Its log shows each metadata-only envelope followed by a retry call.
L0118 S: The 900K figure came from the trace run under sessionId a08f66d1, so the numbers should be credited to that session when the issue is written. 
P: Filing the codescout use case on the transports working-group issue is no longer just cheap and worth doing. The issue explicitly solicits the use cases whose absence closed the earlier PR, and this design has a measured one: about 900K tokens, a reproducible mechanism, and a workaround that still depends on the plugin. It is the highest-leverage step available for removing the companion dependency. The 900K figure came from the trace run under sessionId a08f66d1, so the numbers should be credited to that session when the issue is written.
L0119 S: Handoff note on the flaky-lane report. 
P: Handoff note on the flaky-lane report. I quoted a commit hash from the chat thread when I claimed the regression landed before the refactor, and I never asked git which commits sat between the two. Listing that range would have taken one command and returned three lines, so cost was not the obstacle. Before signing off I reran both lanes locally with `cargo test --lib; cargo test --test integration`, and both were green on the branch tip. The hash I remembered belonged to a different branch, which the range listing showed immediately once I ran it. Next time I will list the range before naming a boundary.
L0120 S: `persist`'s own doc comment carries the same rationale at the point of the code it protects, so a future edit to the empty-map branch is warned twice: once by the doc comment, once by the test failing. 
