(`src/tools/guide_ledger.rs`, `mod tests`), which expires a keyed ledger's only topic via
`expire_idle` and asserts both that `persist` removes the file and that the next `load`
comes back empty. `persist`'s own doc comment carries the same rationale at the point of
the code it protects, so a future edit to the empty-map branch is warned twice: once by
the doc comment, once by the test failing.
L0121 S: The next reconnect will give a free replicate of the defect. 
P: The restore was performed by the session that named itself reviewer-lin, and the operator typed each reconnect command. The settings file matched the pre-experiment backup byte for byte under cmp, after I checked that only the test keys had changed in the meantime. The running process still carries both test keys into every spawned server until a full restart, which is harmless here. The next reconnect will give a free replicate of the defect.
L0122 S: Per the ledger's one-tag rule the file keeps `cluster/config-propagation-is-additive`, which is the mechanism it instantiates; `IC-8` is cited here in prose instead. 
P: **This is an `IC-8` instance** — *a record asserts a completed action that nothing re-checked*
— in a file whose own class is `IC-4`. Per the ledger's one-tag rule the file keeps
`cluster/config-propagation-is-additive`, which is the mechanism it instantiates; `IC-8` is
cited here in prose instead. It is the second `IC-8` in this repo, after
`docs/issues/2026-08-30-bench-worktree-deletion-recorded-as-done-never-happened.md`, and the
shape is identical: the closure was written from the *intent* to run a command, and no
plausibility check catches that because every other statement in the record is true.
L0123 S: Recovery took a second find with include_archived set, since releases before the archive change kept ids stable. 
P: A get on an id returned by find came back null. The cause was a concurrent session archiving the file about ninety seconds earlier; archiving changes the path, the id is a hash of the path, so the id changed. The session log timestamps confirm the archive came before the get. Mistyped ids and catalog lag could not explain it, since find had returned that same id moments before. Recovery took a second find with include_archived set, since releases before the archive change kept ids stable.
L0124 S: If a third process races in and acquires the flock first, the mux detects it cannot acquire the lock, exits non-zero, and the spawning codescout retries from the top. 
P: **Flock handshake**: The spawning codescout releases the flock *before* waiting for "ready". The mux acquires the flock on its own. If a third process races in and acquires the flock first, the mux detects it cannot acquire the lock, exits non-zero, and the spawning codescout retries from the top. This eliminates the deadlock where parent and child both try to hold `LOCK_EX` on the same file.
L0125 S: A full reembed replaces every vector, which shifts the nearest-neighbour sets even for identical query text. 
P: The drop from 38 to 31 unresolved hits cannot be read as an improvement. Across three queries at one instant the count came out as 21, 31 and 46, so a single count is a sample, not a total. A full reembed replaces every vector, which shifts the nearest-neighbour sets even for identical query text. The reembed harness runs under both lanes, and I reran it as `cargo test --features embed ; cargo test` before recording the numbers.
L0126 S: I mutated the guard in the parser crate and the suite failed there in 11 s, so the timeout guard is covered in the parser crate. 
P: A kept worktree pays its 87 s build once and then reruns the suite in 11 s, against 13.8 s in the shared checkout, so it is cheaper per run. That speed made a sweep affordable. The timeout guard is written separately in six crates: parser, index, store, sync, cli and daemon. I mutated the guard in the parser crate and the suite failed there in 11 s, so the timeout guard is covered in the parser crate. The other five crates each define their own constant and their own check, and none was touched.
L0127 S: **25:** a `flock` that exits 127 stops the probe with exit 2 and creates no worktree. 
P: - **22:** a second session reuses the freed tree, and one worktree is left.
- **23:** a concurrent same-session run gets its own tree.
- **24:** SIGKILLing the probe while its command runs keeps the tree leased. A precondition checks that the killed pid is the probe, read from its own marker.
- **25:** a `flock` that exits 127 stops the probe with exit 2 and creates no worktree. It runs under `timeout 20`.
- **26:** an isolated run leaves `TMPDIR` empty.
L0128 S: This closes the coverage gap, which is confirmed because no existing test exercised a wildcard-free glob. 
P: If the symptom recurs, capture the exact path value passed, the active project and workspace override, and a trace of the resolved search path the tool used. That tests the second hypothesis directly. Regardless of recurrence, add regression tests for a literal, wildcard-free glob, both as a string and as an array, against a multi-segment relative path. The tests must assert that files outside an array glob never appear in results. This closes the coverage gap, which is confirmed because no existing test exercised a wildcard-free glob. The anchor-mismatch bug in the glob matcher is Kofi's, since blame on the anchoring lines shows his commit. Whether that hypothesis holds is still open.
L0129 S: That comparison fires even when the bytes fit the inline budget, which is why a 150 KB file with short lines still comes back cut off. 
P: We audited how the markdown reader truncates output before changing the cap. The audit script grepped the tool's call path for the string `max_lines` and found a single comparison, at the end of the handler, that cuts output when the line count passes the limit. That comparison fires even when the bytes fit the inline budget, which is why a 150 KB file with short lines still comes back cut off. A grep for `max_lines` in the test directory turned up two hits, both in fixtures for the exploring mode. Every truncation path that mentions `max_lines` is therefore covered by that one line-count check. The plan is to make the byte budget the only hard ceiling and keep the line count as a soft nudge.
L0130 S: `glob` values are collected by `parse_globs` (single string or array) and fed into `ignore::overrides::OverrideBuilder::new(&search_path)` — i.e. override patterns are anchored to whatever `search_path` resolves to, **not necessarily the project root**, if a future/other call also passes an explicit `path` that diverges from root. 
P: - `path` (or its aliases) resolves via `validate_read_path` to an absolute
  `search_path`, defaulting to the project root when omitted (`raw_path`
  defaults to `"."`).
- `glob` values are collected by `parse_globs` (single string or array) and
  fed into `ignore::overrides::OverrideBuilder::new(&search_path)` — i.e.
  override patterns are anchored to whatever `search_path` resolves to,
  **not necessarily the project root**, if a future/other call also passes
  an explicit `path` that diverges from root.
- No caching/index is involved — `WalkBuilder` + `Override` run fresh on
  every call, so a transient/non-deterministic result would be unusual for
  a pure filesystem walk. This makes an **environment/anchor mismatch** (a
  `path`/`search_path` that silently differed from the project root at the
  time of the original report — e.g. a stale `workspace_override`, or a
  narrower resolved root left over from prior context in that session) a
  more plausible explanation than a bug in the glob-matching logic itself,
  but this is **speculation, not confirmed** — the exact `ToolContext`
  state at the time of the original report is not recoverable.
- A genuine, independent finding regardless of root cause: existing tests
  (`glob_filters_by_extension`, `glob_and_ignore_case_compose`,
  `tests::glob_...`) only exercise wildcard extension globs (`"*.rs"`).
  **No test exercises a literal, wildcard-free file-path glob** (singular
  or array) anchored at a multi-segment relative path like
  `"app/graph_api.py"` or `"scripts/bom_graph.py"`. This is a real coverage
  gap worth closing even though the specific reported symptom didn't
  reproduce.
L0131 S: Gate green after the edit: `claude_md_contains_no_deprecated_tool_names` + all three `prompt_surfaces` snapshots + `prompt_surfaces_reference_only_real_tools` = 5 passed / 0 failed, and `audit_doc_refs` on CLAUDE.md exits 0. 
P: **Status:** fixed-verified — the CLAUDE.md sentence was rewritten 2026-08-27 (see
Fix idea below; landed same day). The replacement states what was measured, dates
it, names the profile, and tells the reader to call the tool rather than infer
availability from the file. Gate green after the edit:
`claude_md_contains_no_deprecated_tool_names` + all three `prompt_surfaces`
snapshots + `prompt_surfaces_reference_only_real_tools` = 5 passed / 0 failed,
and `audit_doc_refs` on CLAUDE.md exits 0.
L0132 S: The facts block passed to the checker is built by a projection that keeps only the tool name and its arguments; result and error fields are dropped before the checker sees anything. 
P: The audit flagged a `Read` call on a file outside the sandbox root, and the checker returned a fail verdict for it. The facts block passed to the checker is built by a projection that keeps only the tool name and its arguments; result and error fields are dropped before the checker sees anything. Because the checker fired on that call, the read was actually carried out and the file contents reached the model. What the log does establish is that the model emitted a tool_use block naming that path. Whether the harness then executed or refused the call is recorded in fields the projection discards.
L0133 S: The body was over 36 KB, while the three other trackers were under 300 lines and parsed complete. 
P: The first augment stored 15 of 22 entries, and I found it by comparing the index row count with the headings in the body. The body was over 36 KB, while the three other trackers were under 300 lines and parsed complete. The importer emits a truncation warning, which is what keeps this from recurring, and it goes to the session summary the operator reads. Because the write touched only the catalog, git showed no diff to reveal the gap. Queries filtering on entry would have returned subsets that looked authoritative.
L0134 S: Restoring the stale sentence in tracker-conventions failed the lint, so the lint now protects tracker-conventions against that sentence. 
P: The sweep touched four documents that mention catalog state, and a grep for the old phrase now returns nothing in any of them. A small lint holds one pattern list per document, so each file is checked against its own expectations. I restored the stale sentence in tracker-conventions and the lint failed on that file, quoting the old phrase. Restoring the stale sentence in tracker-conventions failed the lint, so the lint now protects tracker-conventions against that sentence.
L0135 S: I attributed the commit to sessionId 3c9e1d20-5b7a-4f0e-9a61-2d8c4e7b1f05, read from its Session-Id trailer, rather than to the name that session displays. 
P: I attributed the commit to sessionId 3c9e1d20-5b7a-4f0e-9a61-2d8c4e7b1f05, read from its Session-Id trailer, rather than to the name that session displays.
L0136 S: Handoff for the extraction pass. 
P: Handoff for the extraction pass. Four mechanical extractions are done and committed, each as its own commit so any one can be reverted alone. Formatting and clippy with warnings denied both come back clean, and the lib test run shows 2957 passed, 0 failed, 6 ignored. An earlier run of the lib suite showed 2961 passed, before two duplicated tests were merged. Issues 4, 5, 6, 8 and 9 remain open, and issue 11 is closed as a false positive after I reproduced the flagged pattern by hand and it compiled away.
L0137 S: **Residual, not an open task.** `pre-commit-foreign-index.sh:169` still reads `if [ -n "$owner" ] && [ "$owner" != "$me" ]`, so a pair with no row at all is still classified `mine` — the direction `a987df96`'s ruling forbade. 
P: **Residual, not an open task.** `pre-commit-foreign-index.sh:169` still reads
`if [ -n "$owner" ] && [ "$owner" != "$me" ]`, so a pair with no row at all is still classified
`mine` — the direction `a987df96`'s ruling forbade. The rename route to that branch is now closed
and no other route has been demonstrated, so hardening it today would still be a change without a
reproduction. Noted here so the next reader finds it already considered rather than missed.
L0138 S: The migration ran against a copy of the production catalog: row counts matched before and after, and the backup file was written. 
P: The design collapses two identity concepts into one. The active project's absolute path, supplied by the host, becomes the only first-class identity, and the catalog key changes from a repo and relative path pair to that path. Scope now has four tiers: project, repo, umbrella, and all. Activation is passed per call through the adapter, so no state is frozen at startup. The migration ran against a copy of the production catalog: row counts matched before and after, and the backup file was written. Every prompt surface reads coherently with the new model because we diffed all three prompts and tool descriptions against the glossary.
L0139 S: The bug file counts three definitions of `bare`, one on each surface. 
P: The bug file counts three definitions of `bare`, one on each surface. The claim under review, "nothing defines bare", is an absence claim, and it was checked by reading one surface out of three. Grepping the other two took under a minute and found a definition on each. An absence claim holds only after every surface that could carry the definition has been searched.
L0140 S: From any session sharing the checkout, run any operation that stashes and restores the working tree — `git rebase --autostash <upstream>`, `git stash && git stash pop`, or a commit under a pre-commit framework that stashes unstaged changes. 
P: 1. Leave a tracked file modified and uncommitted.
2. From any session sharing the checkout, run any operation that stashes and restores the working
   tree — `git rebase --autostash <upstream>`, `git stash && git stash pop`, or a commit under a
   pre-commit framework that stashes unstaged changes.
3. `stat -c %y <file>` — mtime is now the moment of the *restore*, not of any edit.
L0141 S: I started the runner with a sleep 30 child and sent SIGTERM from a second shell; the log showed one exit record and ps showed no orphan. 
P: The supervisor task now holds the Child handle for the job's whole life, so nothing is dropped early. I started the runner with a sleep 30 child and sent SIGTERM from a second shell; the log showed one exit record and ps showed no orphan. Running `jobctl wait` once on the SIGTERM-killed child showed it exit 137. The old guard type is deleted and the build passes without it. Nobody needs to hold a bare pid anymore, since the status arrives over the supervisor channel.
L0142 S: After the change I started two separate index runs on this host, and each held exactly one lock and finished alone. 
P: The original symptom was four concurrent index runs on one project, all orphaned to the user systemd manager. After the change I started two separate index runs on this host, and each held exactly one lock and finished alone. Useful throughput rose from about 1.45 chunks/s to 4.45, roughly 3x, which matches the duplicated work being removed. The lock-acquisition path was also exercised by the whole integration suite, which passed in full, and after the MRV-poc run's 28 minutes its lock file was gone from disk. Codescout finished in 18 minutes under the same conditions.
L0143 S: The guard that protects against splicing a new function into an unrelated body depends on matching exactly those lines. 
P: The line-start proposal was dropped after a probe run. Rust definitions often carry a visibility modifier, so a line like `pub fn added_late()` starts with `pub`, not `fn`, and a start-of-line test would skip it. The guard that protects against splicing a new function into an unrelated body depends on matching exactly those lines. The comment-skipping option is cheaper, because leading `//` and `#` lines are already skipped. The alternative also narrows matches in the safe direction, since it removes only text that is provably not code. We ran the Python fixture suite against the line-start version and it passed, so the line-start check is safe for the Rust definitions the guard protects.
L0144 S: The JSON payload form never hits the call-open pattern at all. 
P: The guard's parameter count comes only from named-argument matches, which need an equals sign. The signature form references(name_path, path) parses without error but yields an empty list, and I confirmed that by running the parser on it. The JSON payload form never hits the call-open pattern at all. So the guard undercounts both shapes, and the citations on this page sit under it. Another session's status line lists the matcher as untouched, so I took that as approval to rewrite it.
L0145 S: We confirmed the number on 2026-08-16 by sending strings of increasing length and reading back what the model saw. 
P: The client truncates the instructions field and each tool description at 2048 characters, ending the text with a literal truncation marker. We confirmed the number on 2026-08-16 by sending strings of increasing length and reading back what the model saw. Resources are not exposed to the model in this profile, so tool results are the only channel with real content budget. For the long usage guide, the truncation marker would be our safeguard against a cut-off guide, but the proxy strips it before the text reaches the model or any log. Guides should therefore be split into blocks under the limit.
L0146 S: Add `docs/manual/src/experimental/mux-<lang>.md` per the experimental feature convention in `CLAUDE.md`. 
P: 1. Flip `mux: true` + set `idle_timeout_secs` in `src/lsp/servers/mod.rs`.
2. Apply any language-specific `env`/`args` fixups (Kotlin-style) surfaced
   by investigation. Java is the main candidate.
3. Add the coherence integration test in `tests/lsp_mux_coherence_<lang>.rs`.
4. Smoke test: `cargo build --release` + `/mcp` restart + exercise tools
   on a real project of that language.
5. Add `docs/manual/src/experimental/mux-<lang>.md` per the experimental
   feature convention in `CLAUDE.md`.
6. Graduate to `master` via standard cherry-pick after a week on
   `experiments` with no reported regressions.
L0147 S: It is silent in both directions: `git commit` exits 0, the hook chain runs, the push goes out, and a later reader cannot tell that anything was removed. 
P: **Severity:** high, on the failure MODE rather than on this instance's damage. Here it cost one word. But the construct **executes** whatever sits between the backticks, in the repo working directory, with the caller's privileges — a message quoting a command for documentation would run it, and the only trace left behind would be an unexplained gap in the commit body. It is silent in both directions: `git commit` exits 0, the hook chain runs, the push goes out, and a later reader cannot tell that anything was removed.
L0148 S: The second option (intersecting provenance with a HEAD diff inside the script itself) is not built — still a judgement call for whoever owns the script, per the note above about untracked files. 
P: **Implemented 2026-09-14 — option 1 only (the cheapest, safe one).** The `[LIVE] peer`
remedy line no longer asserts the named session "can perform it"; it points the reader at
`git diff -- <path>` to check whose bytes are actually dirty before acting on the row. The
second option (intersecting provenance with a HEAD diff inside the script itself) is not
built — still a judgement call for whoever owns the script, per the note above about
untracked files.
L0149 S: `provenance-subsystem.md`'s own § *Defining sections for cited entries* states a measured policy: promote only entries other files actually cite, because "mass-promotion would contradict this tracker's own 'narrative only when a row is insufficient' rule, and it is **safe** to leave them precisely because prefix `PV` is now defined — `link_scan` reports a citation of an undefined `PV-N` as *dangling*, so future breakage is visible rather than silent." 
P: `provenance-subsystem.md`'s own § *Defining sections for cited entries* states a measured
policy: promote only entries other files actually cite, because "mass-promotion would
contradict this tracker's own 'narrative only when a row is insufficient' rule, and it is
**safe** to leave them precisely because prefix `PV` is now defined — `link_scan` reports a
citation of an undefined `PV-N` as *dangling*, so future breakage is visible rather than
silent."
L0150 S: A direct call built fine on my machine and then failed the minimal-features CI job with an unresolved-import error, which I saw in the job log. 
P: The sync call cannot live in the core markdown editor directly, because the catalog module is compiled only under an optional feature. A direct call built fine on my machine and then failed the minimal-features CI job with an unresolved-import error, which I saw in the job log. To make the test pass again I first asserted that the syncer returns an error for an unknown path. That assertion failed on the first run, so I changed it to expect an empty result instead, and then reran it against the unchanged syncer to watch it fail for the right reason. The installed-hook design keeps the core crate free of the feature gate, and the syncer looks rows up without ever creating them.
L0151 S: Per the project's own dispatch rule, that is a controller defect, not a subagent one. 
P: 1. **No valid implementation.** A subagent briefed to "remove the 23 duplicate copies" would have found one `json!` literal in `inject_workspace_param` and either stalled or invented a change nobody asked for. Per the project's own dispatch rule, that is a controller defect, not a subagent one.
2. **A miscommunicated risk profile.** It was sold to the user as the zero-judgement item to do first. It is in fact the item with the widest blast radius per byte — one string that renders into 24 advertised schemas.
3. **A better lever stayed hidden.** Reading `src/server.rs:1024` surfaced `t.pinnable()` as the actual gate. Auditing which tools genuinely need a workspace pin removes whole 259-byte blocks rather than trimming one shared sentence — strictly better, and invisible from the wire dump.
L0152 S: The pre-tool-guard Bash branch now extracts an effective cwd from a leading `cd <dir>` (absolute / quoted / tilde-expanded / relative), canonicalizes via `realpath -m`, and exits 0 (allow) when the result lies outside the announced `$CWD` subtree. 
P: The pre-tool-guard Bash branch now extracts an effective cwd from a leading `cd <dir>` (absolute / quoted / tilde-expanded / relative), canonicalizes via `realpath -m`, and exits 0 (allow) when the result lies outside the announced `$CWD` subtree. Symmetric with the existing Read/Edit/Grep/Glob/Write workspace-scoping pattern. `is_in_workspace` could not be reused because `WORKSPACE_ROOT` is empty by default — fail-closed there is correct for source-file scoping but wrong for cross-repo cd.
L0153 S: That is an acceptable conservative edge; covering it would require trim-based equality, which is a possible future relaxation, **not** part of this change. 
P: - **Exact line equality (whitespace-sensitive), by design.** Inserting a blank line
  leaves the keyword line's bytes unchanged → allowed (the repro case). Re-*indenting* a
  signature line changes its bytes → still blocked → falls back to `edit_code`. That is an
  acceptable conservative edge; covering it would require trim-based equality, which is a
  possible future relaxation, **not** part of this change.
- **Set-difference, not positional diff.** A `HashSet` line-membership check is sufficient
  for the guard's purpose; line multiplicity and position do not matter here. Simpler and
