L0197 S: First, scenario modules share names, so loading them through path insertion lets one shadow another; this is a genuine bug, since two modules imported as the same name returned the first one's functions in my reproduction. 
P: The test-suite gap has three separable pieces. First, scenario modules share names, so loading them through path insertion lets one shadow another; this is a genuine bug, since two modules imported as the same name returned the first one's functions in my reproduction. Second, the permanent-red convention should become a marker plus a deselect, so a fully green run becomes expressible. Third, a CI job over the scenarios directory can follow. Once that job exists, none of the current scenarios will fail silently, because each is collected by it.
L0198 S: They only stop a single empty answer from turning a whole run red. 
P: The flake in symbol lookups is not explained yet, and the two changes we landed do not explain it. They only stop a single empty answer from turning a whole run red. What we did gain is visibility: the offline-languages counter no longer sits pinned at a placeholder value, so a real server that fails to answer now shows up there. To size the problem, I grepped the last week of CI logs for the string "returned None" and found 14 hits across 9 runs. So the flake left a logged "returned None" line in 9 runs in the last week. Whether those 9 share a language server is the next thing to check, and the counter makes that a one-query job.
L0199 S: The remedy is to hoist the preferences read into the caller and pass it in as a parameter, the same way `libraries` already arrives. 
P: The prompt builder opens the embedding database and ensures the vector table synchronously on every render. I confirmed this by reading the build function and both call sites: onboarding and the server-instruction refresh. Nothing caches the connection, and errors are skipped silently, so rendering is tied to the embedding DB lifecycle and pays real I/O latency on a hot path. Before the Phase 5 refactor this read lived in `prompts/legacy_render.rs`, which held a 40-line copy of the same open logic. The remedy is to hoist the preferences read into the caller and pass it in as a parameter, the same way `libraries` already arrives.
L0200 S: The pre-commit guard script decides whether to refuse a commit by checking a stage log kept inside the git directory. 
P: The pre-commit guard script decides whether to refuse a commit by checking a stage log kept inside the git directory. The hook that writes that log ends every error branch with a bare exit 0, which I confirmed by listing each branch with grep and counting eleven of them, all identical. So a transient write failure leaves the log missing without any signal. From the guard's source, when the log file is absent it prints a refusal message and exits 1, so the commit is blocked until the log returns. I have not executed the guard against a missing log. Given the silent failure upstream, the practical risk is a stale or absent log that nobody notices for a long time.
L0201 S: That absence of damage says nothing about safety, though: stash with the untracked flag captures such files and a forced clean removes them. 
P: Three untracked siblings kept their original modification times through both events, which I confirmed by comparing stat output taken before and after. Going by the man page alone, `git clean -n` lists the untracked siblings and exits 0. That absence of damage says nothing about safety, though: stash with the untracked flag captures such files and a forced clean removes them. The siblings were spared only because neither of those operations ran against them.
L0202 S: Neither limitation appears in the docs; the only source is the loader code itself, which the reporter traced line by line. 
P: A host serving CodeRankEmbed through llama-server kept losing large embeds to slot-context drops. The reporter read the ONNX loader and confirmed it has no entry for that model, and the sparse leg of hybrid search is absent there too. Because vectors from the two paths differ, moving over would force a full reindex. The peer session that triaged the host had already told it to switch to ONNX, so the reporter ran the migration on the production index without further checks. Neither limitation appears in the docs; the only source is the loader code itself, which the reporter traced line by line.
L0203 S: Measured instead of accepted: `HEAD..origin/experiments` is **0** and `rb.sh` exits **0**, because those 36 commits came FROM this checkout — origin moved to MATCH local HEAD, not past it. 
P: **A FIFTH INSTANCE, NOT MINE, AND IT MOVES THE DETECTION EARLIER.** sessionId `9e022ef0`
reported that this push would make `scripts/rb.sh` *"fire on trees that were fine ten
minutes ago"*, reasoning from *"origin advanced 36 commits"* to *"therefore local is
behind"*. Measured instead of accepted: `HEAD..origin/experiments` is **0** and `rb.sh`
exits **0**, because those 36 commits came FROM this checkout — origin moved to MATCH local
HEAD, not past it. **"Origin advanced" and "you are behind" are different relations, and a
push that publishes local work produces only the first.** Their statement is exactly right
about a *different clone*, which did gain 36 commits it lacks.
L0204 S: **This is `IC-6`, and it is the half named in `CLAUDE.md` § *Parsers Over a Namespace*: a grammar over a namespace with no way to MENTION its own token.** The section's own example is *"an entry id 
P: 171 refs restored to the gate, and 29 broken ones became visible having been hidden since 2026-09-02. None is `high`; `exit_code` stays `0`, so CI was green before and is green after — it was green for the wrong reason. **This is `IC-6`, and it is the half named in `CLAUDE.md` § *Parsers Over a Namespace*: a grammar over a namespace with no way to MENTION its own token.** The section's own example is *"an entry id
L0205 S: I ran the scenario suite twice against the Codex harness and both runs failed the same criterion, the one demanding a native skill invocation. 
P: Handoff note on the acceptance-criteria grader. I ran the scenario suite twice against the Codex harness and both runs failed the same criterion, the one demanding a native skill invocation. The transcript shows Codex opening the skill file through a shell grep, so the criterion rejects a legitimate path. The severity-floor check held up: a minor style comment never counted as a finding in either run. From reading the grader's prompt template, the semantic grader returns a bare PASS or FAIL string with no reasoning field when a criterion is rejected. Next step is to widen the criterion so it accepts either loading method.

