L0234 S: **Why the belief was reasonable, which is the part a future reader needs.** `doc(action="move")` *does* perform automatic repair and reports it: every move response carries `history_grafted: {events, observations, links, event_edges}` from `graft.rs::repoint_history`. 
P: **Why the belief was reasonable, which is the part a future reader needs.**
`doc(action="move")` *does* perform automatic repair and reports it: every move response
carries `history_grafted: {events, observations, links, event_edges}` from
`graft.rs::repoint_history`. That repair is real and it covers **catalog rows**. It does
not touch **prose** — and the `cites` edges it re-points are themselves *derived from
prose* by `link_scan`, so stale prose is re-derived into stale edges on the next scan.
The visible automatic repair sits upstream of the thing that stays broken, which is why
seeing a move report N links re-pointed supports exactly the wrong inference.
L0235 S: Another candidate, that the doctor count derives from truncated rows, died when the reviewer ran doctor on a 300-row fixture and saw the count read 300 while only the total was cut at 200. 
P: The refutation passes killed real candidates. One was a premise the controller seeded itself: that the temp-path strings mismatch on form. The reviewer read the tokenizer's source and concluded that shell_words strips quotes at runtime, so the match survives, without feeding it a single quoted path. Another candidate, that the doctor count derives from truncated rows, died when the reviewer ran doctor on a 300-row fixture and saw the count read 300 while only the total was cut at 200. Three findings were also duplicated across independent reviewers.
L0236 S: The guard: `if body_changing && !a.force && original.len() >= SHRINK_GUARD_MIN_BYTES` (`update.rs:342`, `SHRINK_GUARD_MIN_BYTES = 200` at `update.rs:70`) `{ ... if !allow_history_trim && new_content.len() * 2 < original.len() { return Err(...) } }` (`update.rs:347`). 
