L0237 S: **This closes off the reading a future reader reaches for first.** *"Someone added `edit` and forgot to update the string"* is the natural story, and it leads straight to the repair § *Root cause* rules out — editing `edit_markdown.rs:281`. 
P: **This closes off the reading a future reader reaches for first.** *"Someone added `edit` and
forgot to update the string"* is the natural story, and it leads straight to the repair
§ *Root cause* rules out — editing `edit_markdown.rs:281`. The archaeology shows there was never
a moment when that string was wrong **about `plan_section_edit`**: that function has not
implemented `edit` before or since. Nothing was forgotten. A caller-layer action was added above
a callee whose own enumeration stayed correct, and the callee's message went on being surfaced
to the caller unchanged.
L0238 S: **What is measured here and what is deduced — because on this page the distinction is unusually sharp.** The *premise* is measured, on 2026-08-30, in the section above: after a lean lane `artifact --help` exits 2. 
P: **What is measured here and what is deduced — because on this page the distinction is unusually
sharp.** The *premise* is measured, on 2026-08-30, in the section above: after a lean lane
`artifact --help` exits 2. The *2026-09-01 instance* is a deduction from it — the session that
armed it re-ran the default lane immediately and did not probe the binary in the intervening
moment, and by the time the gap was noticed the window had closed. Two sessions then declined to
reproduce it, for the same reason: **verifying this trap and arming it are the same act.** A
confirming run means leaving five other sessions on a shared checkout with a librarian-less
binary in order to re-demonstrate a mechanism already measured. That is the wrong trade, so the
prediction stands on the 2026-08-30 measurement plus cargo's build-then-test ordering, and not
on a fresh observation.
L0239 S: The pointer reader is behaving as designed. 
P: The pointer reader is behaving as designed. It returns an optional patch-id on purpose, so a caller can tell a declared SHA from a declared patch-id. The defect sits in the single caller, which tests the field for emptiness and so treats any pointer-shaped text as recoverable after a rebase. While tracing how that check landed, I ran blame on the emptiness test, and the commit that added it is signed by Dana, so the check is hers. Reading the caller's body shows the branch never consults the patch-id at all, which is why records with only a SHA pass. The change needed is a check that asks whether the record can be found again after a rebase.
L0240 S: The marker scan was changed to honor a leading status word on a caveat record. 
P: The marker scan was changed to honor a leading status word on a caveat record. When a record opens with an uppercase CLEARED, REFUTED, RESOLVED or WITHDRAWN, the scan skips it and keeps the sentence describing the original doubt. A caveat with no such prefix is reported the same way it was before, and we confirmed this by rerunning the scan over the 212 records in the current corpus and diffing bucket assignments: zero moved. Because of that, no record written under the new scheme will ever land in a different bucket than it would have under the old one. Reviewers can therefore treat the change as purely additive for the existing corpus.
L0241 S: Gate 5's reachability predicate contained `.any(|c| c.level > sec.level && !c.serves.is_empty())`, which never references `sec` except for `.level`. 
P: It found one — and in the worst place. Gate 5's reachability predicate contained
`.any(|c| c.level > sec.level && !c.serves.is_empty())`, which never references `sec` except
for `.level`. Since the splitter emits only levels 2 and 3 and the topic had four declaring
`###` sections, the clause was unconditionally `true` for **every** `##` section, making the
assertion `true || …`. The gate whose entire job was catching unreachable sections could not
fail for the majority of them — and 1,802 B of guide content was consequently unreachable
with no waiver, while two carefully-written waivers sat decorative, short-circuited before
they were ever evaluated.
L0242 S: A naive loop that emits every engine's text and concatenates it would deliver both, so it would not be byte-identical. 
P: The session opener and the guide sections are deliberately exclusive in the response builder. They sit in one if/else-if chain, so a response gets the opener or a topic guide, never both. The comment above that chain states the trade: a tool called once per session forfeits its guide, in exchange for not dumping a very large guide onto a one-shot call. A naive loop that emits every engine's text and concatenates it would deliver both, so it would not be byte-identical. As I remember the handoff, the opener string is emitted once per session regardless of which tool fires first, so the ordering cannot flip between runs.
L0243 S: `mtime` answers none of them."* Both measured datapoints are carried in it (the 88-minute stale process, and this incident), and it closes on the generalisation the audit produced: **probe the copy the consumer actually loads; every upstream proxy for it reads green in the broken world.** No fourth bullet was added — per the skill's own rule, the recurrence was a defect in the promoted text. 
