now opens: *"Freshness is a property of the copy that SERVES you, and it breaks on three
independent axes — build, process, and distribution. `mtime` answers none of them."* Both
measured datapoints are carried in it (the 88-minute stale process, and this incident), and
it closes on the generalisation the audit produced: **probe the copy the consumer actually
loads; every upstream proxy for it reads green in the broken world.** No fourth bullet was
added — per the skill's own rule, the recurrence was a defect in the promoted text.
L0244 S: The lint gate's coverage was checked after the change to its file-type filter. 
P: The lint gate's coverage was checked after the change to its file-type filter. Before the change, it visited only a handful of the hook scripts, and none of the ones written as ES modules, which hold the message text it is meant to police. After adding the module extension, a rerun listed every script in the folder. We also added a comment scrubber for the module files, because the hooks mention Rust function names in comments and those would otherwise trip the identifier check. A grep for snake_case tokens outside comments returned nine hits, so those nine are the identifiers the gate now sees in the hooks directory.
L0245 S: The reindex tool call times out on the client side for large catalogs. 
P: The reindex tool call times out on the client side for large catalogs. In the earlier trial, the server kept working after the client dropped the connection, and the vector store held every expected row afterwards. Because the `archive_2019` row count in the vector store matches its `catalog_meta` entry, its reindex must have completed, so no one needs to look at it. For any catalog, the dependable check is to read `catalog_meta` and count rows in the vector store, not to trust the tool's response envelope. Durable progress in `catalog_meta` would remove the need for that manual step, and that work is still open.
L0246 S: The root `.env` held an absolute path left over from an old machine, while `.env.gpu` and `.env.cpu` both point at `./models`. 
P: The root `.env` held an absolute path left over from an old machine, while `.env.gpu` and `.env.cpu` both point at `./models`. Compose prefers the invoking process's environment over `.env`, so an exported variable fully masks the stale value; the config docs and a quick test with an empty shell both confirm this order. Shell history from session b52e07d1 shows the export as its first command, and session 9e13aa60 did the same. The session labeled `gpu-bringup` exported the variable in every shell it opened, which hid the stale value. Only a run from a clean environment finally exposed it.
L0247 S: A rerun with the HEAD guard disabled exited 0, so the files were written without error. 
P: The report is a frozen snapshot taken at the starting commit, not a statement about the tree as it stands now. The first run exited 2 because shared HEAD advanced during collection. A rerun with the HEAD guard disabled exited 0, so the files were written without error. The archive listing shows every source file was materialized from the starting commit, and the history query log records the exact SHA it used. The guard failure from the first run stays in the record.
L0248 S: I listed the refs on the remote this morning: experiments sits exactly at the tip of the last merge into master, so nothing has drifted. 
P: Handoff for the branch cleanup. I listed the refs on the remote this morning: experiments sits exactly at the tip of the last merge into master, so nothing has drifted. Reading the pre-push hook script shows it rejects any direct commit to master. Nobody should recreate experiments, and git reflog shows no force-reset since that merge. Before the next cherry-pick, someone needs to run the frog audit over the pending commits, since the documentation gap list is two weeks stale.
L0249 S: Two sessions edited files in src/tools/ this morning, so I attributed the change to scheduler.rs from the Session-Id trailer on the commit that made it. 
P: Two sessions edited files in src/tools/ this morning, so I attributed the change to scheduler.rs from the Session-Id trailer on the commit that made it.
L0250 S: It fails when that wording is deleted, which I confirmed by removing the line locally. 
P: The added assertion sits in the existing PEER case of the formatter test script and checks that the remedy text says the peer is not guaranteed to hold anything to format. It fails when that wording is deleted, which I confirmed by removing the line locally. It does not test whether the peer can perform the remedy. That case only exercises the default text output. The new assertion passes against the default text output, so the PEER remedy wording is present there.
L0251 S: That is why the embedding backlog stayed empty after the first run: the queue log shows zero enqueue events between the walk start and walk end. 
P: The reindex flag semantics caused confusion during the handoff. Passing force makes the walker ignore cached file hashes and re-classify every file, but it does not queue anything for embedding. That is why the embedding backlog stayed empty after the first run: the queue log shows zero enqueue events between the walk start and walk end. The reembed option is the one that queues unchanged files, and it matters after switching backends on an already-indexed project. The stalled backlog was traced to the session called "nightly-indexer", since that name appears on the config edit in the audit log. Until reembed is passed, unchanged content is silently never embedded.
