L0258 S: A pure function compares that list against the configured fields and names those where an injected value won, and the resolver warns for each one. 
P: Provenance is now recorded so overrides can be told apart. The resolver reads three sources: the config file, the process environment, and a dotenv file loaded at startup. The startup routine already knew exactly which keys it injected and used to discard the list; it is now stored in a global and read once by the embed environment builder. A pure function compares that list against the configured fields and names those where an injected value won, and the resolver warns for each one. Dotenv is the only source that will ever shadow a configured field, so every future override is covered by this warning.
L0259 S: **`124` needs a policy slot next to `141`.** A timed-out stage exits 124 and its downstream neighbours then see a clean EOF and exit 0 — so the pipeline looks successful unless `PIPESTATUS` is read. 
P: - **`timeout` is GNU coreutils and its presence on Windows Git Bash is NOT verified here.** The
  same package supplies `grep`/`head`/`tail`/`sed`, which `src/platform/windows.rs:182-193`
  already depends on by design — so it is *likely* present, and likely is not measured. Run
  `<git-bash> -c 'command -v timeout'` on a Windows host before relying on it. If absent,
  per-stage timeout degrades to unavailable-on-Windows rather than unavailable-everywhere, and
  total timeout still works on both.
- **`124` needs a policy slot next to `141`.** A timed-out stage exits 124 and its downstream
  neighbours then see a clean EOF and exit 0 — so the pipeline looks successful unless
  `PIPESTATUS` is read. That is the same reason the block above rules against bare
  `set -o pipefail`; the two findings compose into one rule: **decide from `PIPESTATUS`, never
  from the pipeline's aggregate status.**
