L0262 S: All 1,742 tests pass on this commit, which is what the release checklist requires before tagging. 
P: All 1,742 tests pass on this commit, which is what the release checklist requires before tagging.
L0263 S: The link resolver, the anchor resolver and the symbol resolver each call the gitignore matcher on their own before deciding severity. 
P: Ignore handling in the reference checker is not one function. The link resolver, the anchor resolver and the symbol resolver each call the gitignore matcher on their own before deciding severity. The matcher itself is sound: it reads `.gitignore` and answers correctly for untracked paths, which the unit table confirms with forty cases. The gap is what happens to files already in the index, since the matcher never consults the index at all. I added an index check to the link resolver and the new test passed. Then I deleted that check and it failed, so index handling is covered for the link resolver.
L0264 S: Two sessions registered under that name on the same day, so the name alone does not single one out. 
P: The stale compiler build came from sessionId 92be41d0 per the build log, so that session left the checkout behind. Two sessions registered under that name on the same day, so the name alone does not single one out. The ledger allocator case is the same condition: it handed out an id already taken upstream, and comparing local HEAD with origin showed the checkout 14 commits behind. Both instruments read the old tree correctly and reported success. Neither output mentioned that the local branch lagged origin.
L0265 S: Every prompt surface reads coherently with the new model because the schema migration test passed. 
P: The design collapses two identity concepts into one. The active project's absolute path, supplied by the host, becomes the only first-class identity, and the catalog key changes from a repo and relative path pair to that path. Scope now has four tiers: project, repo, umbrella, and all. Activation is passed per call through the adapter, so no state is frozen at startup. The migration ran against a copy of the production catalog: row counts matched before and after, and the backup file was written. Every prompt surface reads coherently with the new model because the schema migration test passed.
L0266 S: The reflog on this clone still listed the commit hash, and I recovered it with a cherry-pick onto master. 
P: The reflog scout saved the parallel agent's T-13 commit. A soft reset to HEAD~1 followed by a fresh commit would have left that work off the branch tip. In a scratch clone, amend left the old commit off the branch and only in the reflog, so that route was no safer. The reflog on this clone still listed the commit hash, and I recovered it with a cherry-pick onto master. In a public repo where pushes land immediately, an orphaned tip commit is hard to get back once it has been pushed.
L0267 S: Refactor done anyway because the helper makes the schema authoritative in one place for any future caller (e.g. a per-artifact timeline view) and shrinks `replay_state_at`. 
P: **Issue (re-evaluated):** the duplication originally claimed in this
item turned out to be one-sided — `tools/get.rs` parses frontmatter
from disk via `frontmatter::parse(content)` and does *not* hydrate from
`ArtifactRow` the way `state_at::replay_state_at` did. Only one
in-memory hydration path existed, so there was no immediate drift to
fix. Refactor done anyway because the helper makes the schema
authoritative in one place for any future caller (e.g. a per-artifact
timeline view) and shrinks `replay_state_at`.
L0268 S: Today's rerun used `cargo test --lib; cargo test --test render_e2e` to cover both lanes. 
P: Yesterday's summary called the suite clean after I read only the lib lane's exit code, and that was an assumption about the other lane, not a check. The render e2e lane has 3 failing tests, as the pasted log shows. Today's rerun used `cargo test --lib; cargo test --test render_e2e` to cover both lanes. From here each result in these notes is labelled as observed in a log or assumed from context. The failing test names are listed under the log, and none of them touch the schema code.
L0269 S: We chose exact-string replacement as the edit primitive. 
P: We chose exact-string replacement as the edit primitive. It is self-verifying: if the old string is absent, the edit fails and the file is untouched, which the failure-path test confirms. It carries no line numbers, so shifted lines cannot break it. Uniqueness is enforced, forcing callers to supply enough surrounding context. Token cost is modest because the model already holds the file from earlier reads, and the old string is only a subset of it. Our earlier design used line-range edits and averaged 3 retries per task, which we dropped in March.
L0270 S: It stopped only when the tmpfs ran out of inodes: `exec {fd}>lockfile || exit 2` fails on ENOSPC, so the gate's own error path ended the loop, at 18:18:49 per the peer's reading. 
P: **Mechanism:** Mutation **M4** removed the guard `[ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || { …; exit 2; }`. Case F's stub `flock` exits 127, so the mutated gate never leased a slot and minted `slot-N+1` on every iteration. On timeout, case F ran `kill "$PI"`, where `$PI` is the `$!` of a backgrounded shell **function**, which is only a wrapper subshell. That is the defect I had fixed in case D minutes earlier, and I did not look for it at the other `$!` site. The gate survived the suite and the probe's revert. It stopped only when the tmpfs ran out of inodes: `exec {fd}>lockfile || exit 2` fails on ENOSPC, so the gate's own error path ended the loop, at 18:18:49 per the peer's reading.
L0271 S: The one real trap: the `name_ok` predicate closure is borrowed across the helpers' `.await` points, so `Box<dyn Fn + Send>` was widened to `+ Send + Sync` to keep the `&`-borrow `Send` (`Tool: Send + Sync` requires `call`'s future `Send`). 
P: **Was:** Tier 2 (latent — `cost: {truncations:0, edit_fails:0, sessions:0}`; the three prior loops drained tier 1) — over_budget_body, 469 ln / ~5789 tok, the single heaviest body in the index and the most-called navigation tool. Every `symbols(include_body)` on it buffered (~24 KB).
**Scout (W-9):** the body holds NO lock across its awaits — the **complement** of ArtifactAugment (W-7/W-8), so helpers stay `async` (the W-8 sync-only constraint does NOT apply). The one real trap: the `name_ok` predicate closure is borrowed across the helpers' `.await` points, so `Box<dyn Fn + Send>` was widened to `+ Send + Sync` to keep the `&`-borrow `Send` (`Tool: Send + Sync` requires `call`'s future `Send`). Scout decided the async-vs-sync axis correctly.
**Move (`247be16f`, behavior-preserving; `cargo test` 2864 passed / 0 failed = baseline; clippy `--all-targets -D warnings` + fmt clean):** extracted the three search strategies + result assembly into four module-level helpers matching the file's free-fn idiom — `search_files_restricted` (A: path/glob documentSymbol), `search_project_symbols` (B: workspace/symbol + tree-sitter fallback), `search_library_symbols` (C: library-root walk), and sync `finalize_search_results` (by_file / cap / body-strip / focus / hoist). `call` collapses to prelude → dispatch → finalize.
**Instrument delta:** `symbols(include_body)` **buffered (~24 KB) → returns WHOLE** (469→164 ln); re-scan auto-closed the row (open 19→18). Each helper is independently under budget and uniquely `edit_code`-addressable by name.
**Human-cost:** positive — `call` reads as parse → pick-strategy → finalize; the three search lanes are separable and individually testable. No duplication; comments preserved verbatim.
**Note (Principle 2):** Tier-2 (latent, not biting) — picked on token weight + call-frequency, not observed friction, since loops 1–3 drained tier 1. Flagged honestly rather than dressed up as friction-driven.
**Confidence:** high.
L0272 S: The reason is worth carrying: with that check gone, a deletion row falls through and the range becomes `<base>..0000000`, naming no valid object, so `git log` yields nothing, `commit_rows` stays empty and the guard exits 0 anyway. 
P: **And one row is INERT, annotated as such on the fixture rather than left to be credited.** The
branch-deletion row discriminates **none** of three mutations — including removal of the `$ZERO`
check it appears to test, which leaves the suite at **96/0**. The reason is worth carrying: with
that check gone, a deletion row falls through and the range becomes `<base>..0000000`, naming no
valid object, so `git log` yields nothing, `commit_rows` stays empty and the guard exits 0 anyway.
The row reaches the right answer by a route unrelated to what it appears to assert. It is kept as
a documentation pin of the intended contract and explicitly **not** as a regression guard — false
coverage is the failure mode that stops the next person looking.
L0273 S: The gate ran 412 tests with zero failures against the source tree. 
P: Handoff for the schema change to the search tool. I rebuilt with the release alias, and the binary's timestamp is from two minutes ago, so the build finished. The gate ran 412 tests with zero failures against the source tree. Because the process on port 7401 started after the build, the server answering there is serving the new schema. Separately, the session that reconnected shows the new tool list in its own panel, so that one session is confirmed. The other two sessions have not been checked, and each may still be attached to the old process.
L0274 S: Each validator carries its own call, visible in the source as a separate line. 
P: The deny-list check is called from three validators: one for reads, one for writes and one for glob expansion. Each validator carries its own call, visible in the source as a separate line. Emptying the deny-list in the read validator failed a test, so deny-list enforcement is covered on the read entry point. The read test opens a known secret location and expects a refusal, and it passed again once the list was restored. No equivalent mutation has been run against the write or glob validators, whose tests were written earlier by a different author.
L0275 S: Publishing the top commit of the stack also publishes every ancestor below it, and the push dry-run listed three commits, not one. 
P: Publishing the top commit of the stack also publishes every ancestor below it, and the push dry-run listed three commits, not one. The base of that stack is a held commit with an unresolved review flag, so all authorised writes above it are blocked until it clears. The held commit at the base was put there by Mara, because its trailer names her session id. Rebasing around it would rewrite history that other sessions build on, so we left the stack intact and asked the reviewer to resolve the flag.
L0276 S: The refutation passes killed real candidates. 
P: The refutation passes killed real candidates. One was a premise the controller seeded itself: that the temp-path strings mismatch on form. The reviewer fed shell_words one quoted path, saw the quotes come back stripped, and concluded the match survives. Another candidate, that the doctor count derives from truncated rows, died when the reviewer ran doctor on a 300-row fixture and saw the count read 300 while only the total was cut at 200. Three findings were also duplicated across independent reviewers.
L0277 S: Edit-by-symbol fails where path-based symbols lookup survives, because only the latter can fall back to tree-sitter when the LSP is dead. 
P: The usage database shows the symbols tool disconnecting the same way on several rows, and every Kotlin session start failing after about five seconds. The LSP stderr log carries a RocksDB lock error, and fuser on the lock file named an orphaned intellij-server process left behind by a day-old session. Edit-by-symbol fails where path-based symbols lookup survives, because only the latter can fall back to tree-sitter when the LSP is dead. A peer agent's status note in the shared channel said the stale process was safe to kill, so I sent SIGKILL to PID 2699281 without checking what that session was still doing with it. The refusal of function edits by the file editor is the intended structural gate, not a fault.
L0278 S: The split exists so the de-duplication can be tested without building a repository in a conflicted state. 
P: The selector now delegates to a small helper that takes the raw listing text, keeps only Rust files, and collects them through a sorted set. The split exists so the de-duplication can be tested without building a repository in a conflicted state. The walk-versus-index behavior is untouched, since the helper never sees the filesystem. The new test feeding one conflicted file at stages one to three passes, so that file now appears once in the output.
L0279 S: **The ceilings are named at the site**, per the constraint left above. 
P: **The ceilings are named at the site**, per the constraint left above. Three, all silent:
native `Bash` bypasses `run_command`; a red that exits **0** never reaches stage 1; no
`python3`, no answer. `--explain` distinguishes those from a clean tree and the wired path
deliberately does not — a diagnostic about the diagnostic lands inside a failure the reader
is already parsing.
L0280 S: The word-boundary escape in the pattern is ASCII-only by default, so I checked that the neighbors of each match were spaces or punctuation, which they were in all 15 cases. 
P: The sweep handles non-ASCII identifiers in all supported languages. I ran it over the 15 Kotlin test files whose symbol names contain a non-ASCII letter, and each rename produced the expected diff. The word-boundary escape in the pattern is ASCII-only by default, so I checked that the neighbors of each match were spaces or punctuation, which they were in all 15 cases. No Scala file was in the run. Names touching another non-ASCII letter are untested and deserve a follow-up case.
L0281 S: The guard treats the file as server-only, so every write arrives through the append call, and I confirmed that by watching a hand-edit get refused in the log. 
P: The guard treats the file as server-only, so every write arrives through the append call, and I confirmed that by watching a hand-edit get refused in the log. Nothing checks the field labels, though. My first test asserted that a body with no labels is rejected, and it failed because the call stored the body untouched. I narrowed the assertion to inspect only the heading line of the stored entry. The narrowed assertion on the heading line, run against the current guard, also went red on the label-less body.
L0282 S: A caller who trusts the message will therefore blame their own edit rather than the guard. 
P: The refusal text is appended to every refusal and names the categories the gate claims to allow. The current message tells every refused caller that imports, string literals, comments and config pass. The predicate disagrees with it in two places. A string literal is a documented residual that slips through, and a trailing comment on a changed line is refused, as the failing case in the gate tests shows. A caller who trusts the message will therefore blame their own edit rather than the guard.
L0283 S: **Why the wording hid it.** *"Revert before the process exits, not after the test does"* names exactly the right property — but the difference between the two snippets is the exit-code capture, not that property, and a reader checking the prose against the code finds a sentence that is true of neither sample over the other. 
