L0294 S: Before the change the reader spun at full CPU; a 30-second capture showed about 400k polls, and the same capture after the change shows about 30k. 
P: The stdin wrapper now arms a 1 ms sleep on EAGAIN and polls it, so the waker is registered through tokio's timer reactor and the task resumes after the delay. Before the change the reader spun at full CPU; a 30-second capture showed about 400k polls, and the same capture after the change shows about 30k. The retry path's log level dropped from WARN to TRACE, which removed the flood from debug.log. I checked the result with `cargo test --lib ; cargo test --test integration` from the crate root.
L0295 S: Recover with `git checkout <target-branch> && git merge --ff-only <recovered-sha>` in a single command. 
P: **Pattern:**
1. Read full state in a single command — `git reflog -15 && git branch -v && git rev-parse HEAD && git symbolic-ref HEAD` — so the observation set is internally consistent before any write.
2. Recover with `git checkout <target-branch> && git merge --ff-only <recovered-sha>` in a single command. The `--ff-only` flag is the atomic safety: it succeeds silently if `<recovered-sha>` is a strict descendant of the target branch's current tip (history is not fabricated, only the branch pointer moves), and it fails loudly if anything diverged between the read and the write.
L0296 S: A rebuild replaces the file and leaves running servers on the old inode by design, so any peer that has not reconnected is serving pre-rebuild behaviour until it runs `/mcp` — **13 of the 14, not all 14: the remaining one is a mux, which has no session to reconnect and exits by itself past its own idle timeout.** That correction is the whole of what the split changes in this entry, and it is worth noticing WHERE it bit. 
P: **A fact worth separating from the defect:** the stale processes are not themselves a fault. A
rebuild replaces the file and leaves running servers on the old inode by design, so any peer
that has not reconnected is serving pre-rebuild behaviour until it runs `/mcp` — **13 of the 14,
not all 14: the remaining one is a mux, which has no session to reconnect and exits by itself
past its own idle timeout.** That correction is the whole of what the split changes in this
entry, and it is worth noticing WHERE it bit. What is a fault
is an instrument that renders that ordinary state as *"the rebuild did not take"*.
L0297 S: Two separate forced reindex runs returned the same seven unknown sample ids, byte for byte, so the set is stable rather than random. 
P: Two separate forced reindex runs returned the same seven unknown sample ids, byte for byte, so the set is stable rather than random. The project's config, which I opened, force-includes tracker and conversation files past the ignore file, so those are candidate sources for the seven ids. Whatever fails to resolve them may also be keeping the remaining rows out of search results, though that link is untested. I did not query the catalog for the stored paths of those ids, because this session had no tooling for it. Status: deferred, with the catalog query as the next step.
L0298 S: `run_command("mkdir -p /tmp/outside && cp <any .rs> /tmp/outside/x.rs")` — or use any directory outside the active project root. 
P: 1. `run_command("mkdir -p /tmp/outside && cp <any .rs> /tmp/outside/x.rs")` — or use any
