the paired exclusion assertion (`!contains("alpha") && !contains("delta")`), which is the
discriminating half. Written that way deliberately; had it been containment-only it would
have shipped green and guarded nothing.
L0362 S: Reproducing on Windows CI meant running `cargo test --lib; cargo test --test cfg_paths` as a single step. 
P: Reproducing on Windows CI meant running `cargo test --lib; cargo test --test cfg_paths` as a single step. Three config tests hand a `/tmp/...` path to the global dir helper. The helper keeps only paths where is_absolute() is true, and on Windows a bare slash-rooted path lacks a drive letter or UNC prefix, so it counts as rooted but not absolute. The filter drops it, the home fallback is also None in these tests, and the unwrap panics.
L0363 S: The byte ceiling in Task 10 is the only byte accounting in the system, and it is scoped to this engine alone. 
P: The default flip to universal came from Tomas, whose name is on the commit that inverted it. After it, a registry test holds every tool to supplying a selector key, so a tool that returns nothing drops out of both engines rather than only this one. The byte ceiling in Task 10 is the only byte accounting in the system, and it is scoped to this engine alone. That is visible in the code: the total sums only the blocks this engine emits, and the other engine has no counterpart.
L0364 S: The root env file also held a registry token that the profiles never carried, and the copy overwrote it; I confirmed this by diffing the file before and after. 
P: The stale model directory came from a copy step, not from the profile files. Copying a profile over the root env file freezes its values at copy time, and the root file kept a path that the profile had already dropped. I ran grep for the variable name against the two profile files and got zero hits, so neither profile still sets it. The root env file also held a registry token that the profiles never carried, and the copy overwrote it; I confirmed this by diffing the file before and after. Secrets should live in that file alone.
L0365 S: A restore probe has to assert on the quantity that can actually separate good runs from bad ones. 
P: A restore probe has to assert on the quantity that can actually separate good runs from bad ones. Counting restored artifacts only tells you how many restores ran, because nine artifacts pointing at one sidecar still yield nine. Distinct prompts are what tell one sidecar from another, so the probe collects the prompt of every restore into a set and compares the set's size. In the latest run it printed nine restored artifacts and stayed green, so no two artifacts share a sidecar.
