P: The second scout pass compared the runtime hint for `append_entry` against its advertised schema. The source defines `anchor_heading` at line 34, yet the schema omits it, so the hint points at a parameter that exists but is unadvertised. Reading only the schema would have called the hint stale; reading only the source would have missed the omission. Separately, we grepped the injected guide files for the string `relevant_guide_topic` and found one hit in each of two guides, so those two auto-injected guides are now confirmed to fire in live sessions. The two guides measured 23.2 KB and 20.5 KB, which matches the 43.8 KB per session that the baseline predicted.
L0442 S: Nothing needs shipping, and the negative result is recorded so nobody repeats the audit. 
P: Audit A-13 looked for two kinds of misleading language across everything we deliver to the agent: the slices, the guides, the builder code, templates, the generated system prompt and the companion hook text. Every grep hit was read by hand and found benign, such as a retry counter and comments describing research methods. The token figures in the progressive-disclosure guide size tool output and do not count down a context budget. Since the grep found no countdown phrasing, the agent never receives a signal about remaining context at runtime. Nothing needs shipping, and the negative result is recorded so nobody repeats the audit.
L0443 S: The stamped-file refusal in the editor tool is still in place, and the handoff should keep it that way. 
P: The stamped-file refusal in the editor tool is still in place, and the handoff should keep it that way. To find out how often it bites, I grepped the last week of session transcripts for the refusal message and got four hits, all from one session that was editing a changelog. Every one of those edits touched the frontmatter block, so the refusal was justified in each case. The grep pattern matched only the exact error string, which the tool emits when it rejects a whole-file edit. We therefore know that no body-only change hit this refusal in the transcripts searched. If the count grows, the follow-up is to compare each edit's extent against the frontmatter and pass a body-write flag when they do not overlap.
L0444 S: Bisecting over six commits isolated the one that lowered the window threshold, and reverting it restored 41. 
P: The regression in the timing arm traces to Keiko, whose commit carries the threshold change the bisect isolated. Reruns at matched token budgets show injections falling from 41 to 17 per hundred windows, while eligible no-trigger windows held at 212. Bisecting over six commits isolated the one that lowered the window threshold, and reverting it restored 41. The context-only arm did not move, so retrieval is not implicated.
L0445 S: The guard check formerly tested for any id value and was named has_id_field before we renamed it. 
P: The template abstention is not new. Stamping an id subjects a file to the librarian guard, and is_librarian_id on the ADR-{NUMBER} placeholder returns false, so templates are unguarded today. The guard check formerly tested for any id value and was named has_id_field before we renamed it. The repair would have stamped an id into such a template, creating the condition the abstention exists to avoid. The regression test now asserts that a placeholder template is left untouched, and it passes.
L0446 S: Per the after-cherry-pick rule the branch-side original orphans on the next rebase, so `0a955491` is history and `4fad1aa4` is the citation. 
P: Cherry-picked from `0a955491` on `worktree-il3-gate-and-find-lift` (pushed to `origin`).
A fast-forward was **not** available: the two had diverged by one commit each way, because
the bookkeeping commit that recorded this fix (`f273f187`) landed on `experiments` after
the branch was cut. Per the after-cherry-pick rule the branch-side original orphans on the
next rebase, so `0a955491` is history and `4fad1aa4` is the citation.
